es to be a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nIndeed, each row \\(i\\) contains precisely one uncovered square, which must lie in a unique column; the condition on columns forces the column indices to be all distinct, hence a permutation.\n\nThe tiles are axis‑aligned rectangles whose interiors consist only of covered squares; equivalently, each tile corresponds to a pair \\((R,C)\\) where  \n\n\\[\nR\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nare non‑empty intervals of rows and columns, and the rectangle \\(R\\times C\\) contains **no** point of \\(U\\).  \nThus the problem is to partition the set  \n\n\\[\nE=\\bigl(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\bigr)\\setminus U\n\\]\n\ninto the smallest possible number of such “hole‑free’’ rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. A lower bound\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_1=\\{(i,\\pi(i+1))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\\qquad \nS_2=\\{(i+1,\\pi(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(S=S_1\\cup S_2\\).  \nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the two families are disjoint; consequently  \n\n\\[\n|S|=2(n-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll elements of \\(S\\) belong to \\(E\\) (none is uncovered).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle with \\(I,J\\) intervals of rows and columns, respectively, such that \\(R\\cap U=\\varnothing\\).  \nThen \\(R\\) contains **at most one** element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nThere are three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both points lie in \\(S_1\\).**  \n   Then they have the form \\((i,\\pi(i+1))\\) and \\((j,\\pi(j+1))\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \n   Since the rectangle contains the whole row interval \\([i,j]\\) and the column interval \\([\\pi(i+1),\\pi(j+1)]\\) (or the reverse order), it also contains the point \\((i+1,\\pi(i+1))\\), which belongs to \\(