ll of \\(L\\)), then for every row \\(i'\\) of \\(R\\) we must have \\(j<i'\\); otherwise the column interval of \\(R\\) would contain the diagonal cell \\((i',i')\\).  Hence all rows of \\(R\\) satisfy \\(i'>j\\), which forces the entire rectangle to lie inside \\(L\\).  An analogous argument shows that a rectangle containing a cell of \\(U\\) must be wholly contained in \\(U\\).  \n\nThus **every admissible rectangle is entirely contained in either \\(L\\) or \\(U\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) rectangles\n\n*Row‑interval construction.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<i\\},\\qquad\nr_i=\\{(i,j):i<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\ell_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) we place a horizontal \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering \\(\\ell_i\\);  \nif \\(r_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) we place a horizontal \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle covering \\(r_i\\).  \n\nExactly one row has its hole in column 1 (namely \\(i=1\\)), and exactly one row has its hole in column \\(n\\) (namely \\(i=n\\)).  Consequently the total number of non‑empty rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }\\ell_i\\neq\\varnothing}\n+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{rows with }r_i\\neq\\varnothing}\n=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nAll rectangles are disjoint and together cover the whole board except the diagonal holes, so this yields a valid tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n*Explicit “staircase’’ tiling.*  \nEquivalently, partition \\(L\\) by the rectangles  \n\n\\[\nR_k=\\{(i,j):k\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le k-1\\},\\qquad k=2,\\dots ,n,\n\\]\n\nand partition \\(U\\) by  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le k,\\;k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\\qquad k=1,\\dots ,n-1.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(R_k\\) (resp. \\(S_k\\)) is a rectangle lying completely in \\(L\\) (resp. \\(U\\)), the families \\(\\{R_k\\}\\) and \\(\\{S_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover \\(L\\cup U\\).  There are \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectang