q j\\).  \nWithout loss of generality \\(p(i)<p(j)\\).  \nBecause both columns \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\) belong to \\([c,d]\\), the whole interval \\([p(i)-1,p(j)-1]\\) is contained in \\([c,d]\\).  \nSince \\(p(i)\\) satisfies \\(p(i)-1<p(i)\\le p(j)-1\\), we have \\(p(i)\\in[c,d]\\).  \nRow \\(i\\) belongs to \\([a,b]\\); therefore the hole \\((i,p(i))\\in H\\) lies in \\(X\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(X\\) avoids all holes. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f2.**  \n\\(X\\) cannot contain two distinct cells of \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f1.  \nIf \\((i,p(i)+1),(j,p(j)+1)\\in R\\cap X\\) and \\(p(i)>p(j)\\), then \\([p(j)+1,p(i)+1]\\subseteq[c,d]\\) and consequently \\(p(i)\\in[c,d]\\); the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) would belong to \\(X\\). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma\u202f3.**  \n\\(X\\) cannot contain one cell of \\(L\\) and one cell of \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose \\((i,p(i)-1)\\in L\\cap X\\) and \\((j,p(j)+1)\\in R\\cap X\\).  \nBoth columns \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)+1\\) belong to \\([c,d]\\); hence the whole interval between them is contained in \\([c,d]\\).\n\n*Case\u202f1:* \\(p(i)\\le p(j)\\).  \nThen \\(p(i)\\) lies between \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)+1\\), so \\(p(i)\\in[c,d]\\).  \nSince row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\), the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) belongs to \\(X\\), a contradiction.\n\n*Case\u202f2:* \\(p(i)>p(j)\\).  \nIf \\(p(i)=p(j)+1\\) (adjacent columns), then the interval \\([c,d]\\) contains both columns \\(p(j)\\) and \\(p(i)\\); consequently the hole \\((j,p(j))\\) (or \\((i,p(i))\\)) lies in \\(X\\), again a contradiction.\n\nIf \\(p(i)\\ge p(j)+2\\), then the interval \\([c,d]\\) contains the whole set of columns \\(\\{p(j)+1,\\dots ,p(i)-1\\}\\).  \nChoose any column \\(c^{*}\\) with \\(p(j)<c^{*}<p(i)\\); there exists a unique row \\(k\\) such that \\(p(k)=c^{*}\\) (because \\(p\\) is a permutation).  \nSince \\(c^{*}\\in[c,d]\\) and \\(k\\) lies between \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) in the numeric order (otherwise the rectangle would not contain column \\(c^{*}\\) together with both rows \\(i\\) and \\(j\\)), we have \\(k\\in[a,b]\\).  \nThus the hole \\((k,c^{*})\\) belongs to \\(X\\), a contradiction.\n\nIn every case a hole would be contained in \\(X\\); therefore such a rectangle cannot exist. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemmas\u202f1\u20133 we obtain:\n\n> **Corollary.** Every admissible rectangle contains **at most one** element of \\(L\\cup R\\).\n\nSince \\(|L\\cup R|=2n-2\\), any tiling of the board must use at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nX_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares left of the hole in that row.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nX_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares right of the hole in that row.\n\nThe rectangles \\(X_i^{\\text{left}}\\) (for \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\)) and \\(X_i^{\\text{right}}\\) (for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)) are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different rows.  \nTogether they cover every non\u2011hole square of the board.  \nThe total number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left rectangles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right rectangles}}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus a placement with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\nThe lower bound shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing the output, carefully review the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure:\n\n* All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n* Every claim is accompanied by a complete, rigorous proof; no steps rely on intuition or unproved assertions.\n* The logical flow follows the outline given in the summary.\n* No extraneous commentary or alternative attempts appear in the detailed proof.\n* The final answer is presented exactly as `\\boxed{4048}`."
