ast \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering row \\(k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) (length \\(n-k\\)).  \n   - For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering column \\(k\\) and rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) (height \\(n-k\\)).  \n   These \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are disjoint, cover every cell except the diagonal holes, and satisfy the required condition.  \n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound (3) and the explicit construction show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this number is \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---  \n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying “exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column’’ forces the set of uncovered squares to be a permutation matrix.  \nHence there exists a permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\pi(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(number of cells left of the hole in row }i),\n\\]\n\\[\nR_i=n-\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{(number of cells right of the hole in row }i).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor a column \\(j\\) let \\(\\sigma=\\pi^{-1}\\) and define  \n\n\\[\nU_j=\\sigma(j)-1\\qquad\\text{(cells above the hole in column }j),\n\\]\n\\[\nD_j=n-\\sigma(j)\\qquad\\text{(cells below the hole in column }j).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle, i.e. a set of cells  \n\n\\[\nT=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\quad (1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(T\\) must avoid all points of \\(H\\), the following holds:\n\n*For each row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) either \\(d<\\pi(i)\\) (the whole rectangle lies left of the hole in that row) or \\(c>\\pi(i)\\) (it lies right of that hole).*\n\n*For each column \\(j\\in[c,d]\\) either \\(b<\\sigma(j)\\) (the rectangle lies above the hole in that column) or \\(a>\\sigma(j)\\) (it lies below that hole).*\n\nThus each tile belongs to exactly one of the four classes\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{NW}&:\\; d<\\pi(i)\\ \\forall i\\in[a,b]\\ \\text{and}\\ b<\\sigma(j)\\ \\forall j\\in[c,d],\\\\\n\\text{NE}&:\\; c>\\pi(i)\\ \\forall i\\in[a,b]\\ \\text{and}\\ b<\\sigma(j)\\ \\forall j\\in[c,d],\\\\\n\\text{SW}&:\\; d<\\pi(i)\\ \\forall i\\in[a,b]\\ \\text{and}\\ a>\\sigma(j)\\ \\forall j\\in[c,d],\\\\\n\\text{SE}&:\\; c>\\pi(i)\\ \\forall i\\in[a,b]\\ \\text{and}\\ a>\\sigma(j)\\ \\forall j\\in[c,d].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nA=\\#\\text{NW tiles},\\; B=\\#\\text{SW tiles},\\; C=\\#\\text{NE tiles},\\; D=\\#\\text{SE tiles}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1 (Rectangular partition of a Young diagram).  \n\nLet a left‑justified shape consist of rows of lengths \\(a_1\\ge a_2\\ge\\cdots\\ge a_m\\).  \nThe smallest number of axis‑parallel rectangles that partition this shape equals the number of distinct positive values among \\(\\{a_i\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a rectangle covers rows \\(i_1\\le\\cdots\\le i_2\\), its width cannot exceed \\(\\min\\{a_{i_1},\\dots ,a_{i_2}\\}\\); therefore a rectangle can cover only rows whose lengths are equal to that minimum. Consequently at least one rectangle is needed for each distinct row length. Conversely, grouping together all rows that share the same length and taking one rectangle for each group yields a partition with exactly that many rectangles. ∎\n\n---\n\nThe left‑segments \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) form a left‑justified Young diagram: row \\(i\\) contains exactly the cells \\((i,1),\\dots,(i,L_i)\\). By Lemma 1 the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all left‑segments equals the number of distinct positive values among the \\(L_i\\). Since \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\