fore\n\\(\\operatorname{rank}(M_{R,C})=1\\) over the real numbers.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound from matrix rank\n\nSuppose a tiling uses \\(t\\) tiles with row/column sets\n\\((R_1,C_1),\\dots ,(R_t,C_t)\\).  \nBecause the tiles are disjoint, the sum (over the reals) of their\nindicator matrices equals the matrix of all covered squares:\n\n\\[\nA = \\sum_{k=1}^{t} M_{R_k,C_k}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe rank of a sum does not exceed the sum of the ranks:\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\n\\le \\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{R_k,C_k})\n= \\sum_{k=1}^{t}1 = t.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe matrix \\(A=J-I\\) has eigenvalues \\(n-1\\) (once) and \\(-1\\) (with\nmultiplicity \\(n-1\\)); thus it is nonsingular and  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=n.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nCombining (2) and (3) yields  \n\n\\[\nt\\ge n.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible tiling needs at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling that uses exactly \\(n\\) tiles\n\nFor each row index \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq i\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn terms of row/column sets,\n\\(R_i=\\{i\\}\\) and \\(C_i=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{i\\}\\); clearly\n\\(R_i\\cap C_i=\\varnothing\\), so \\(T_i\\) is a legitimate rectangle.\nThe tiles \\(T_1,\\dots ,T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint because a square\n\\((i,j)\\) belongs to exactly the tile \\(T_i\\).  \n\nEvery row \\(i\\) contains the uncovered square \\((i,i)\\) and all other\n\\(n-1\\) squares of that row are covered by \\(T_i\\).  \nSimilarly each column \\(j\\) contains the uncovered square \\((j,j)\\) and the\nremaining \\(n-1\\) squares of that column are covered (by the tiles\n\\(T_i\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\)).  \n\nThus the \\(n\\) tiles satisfy all requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nThe lower bound (4) show