on is a tiling of the diagonal configuration, and vice‑versa.  \nHence the minimal number of tiles depends only on \\(n\\); we may assume from now on that the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal cells (2).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. A tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle; it contains no diagonal cell because its column indices are all \\(>i\\).\n\nFor each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n\\(V_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle, also free of diagonal cells.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nIf \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\) then it belongs to \\(H_i\\) and cannot belong to any \\(V_{j'}\\) because every \\(V_{j'}\\) contains only cells with row index \\(>j'\\).  \nIf \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to \\(V_j\\) and to no \\(H_{i'}\\).  \nThus the sets \\(\\{H_i\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Covering.*  \nEvery off‑diagonal cell satisfies either \\(i<j\\) or \\(i>j\\); consequently it lies in exactly one of the rectangles defined above. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n-1}H_i\\;\\cup\\;\\bigcup_{j=1}^{n-1}V_j\n=G\\setminus\\{(i,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\,|+|\\,\\{V_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\,|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles\n\nWe prove that no tiling of the diagonal configuration can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\nThe set of off‑diagonal cells splits into two disjoint parts:\n\n\\[\nU^{+}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly upper‑triangular)},\n\\]\n\\[\nU^{-}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}\\qquad\\text{(strictly lower‑triangular)}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle that avoids the diagonal must lie entirely in one of these parts, because if it contained a cell with \\(i<j\\) and another with \\(i'>j'\\) then, letting \\(r=\\max\\{i,i'\\}\\) and \\(c=\\min\\{j,j'\\}\\), we would have \\(r\\ge c\\) and the rectangle would contain the diagonal cell \\((c,c)\\), a contradiction.  \nHence every tile belongs either to \\(U^{+}\\) or to \\(U^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1. Upper‑triangular part\n\nConsider the **super‑diagonal** cells  \n\n\\[\nS^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U^{+}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) be a rectangle contained in \\(U^{+}\\).  \nBecause every cell of \\(R\\) satisfies row \\< column, we must have  \n\n\\[\nb<c. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S^{+}\\), say \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(a\\le i<j\\le b\\) and \\(c\\le i+1<j+1\\le d\\).  \nFrom (8) we obtain \\(b<c\\le i+1\\), whence \\(j\\le i\\), contradicting \\(i<j\\).  \nTherefore **any rectangle inside \\(U^{+}\\) contains at most one element of \\(S^{+}\\).**\n\nSince \\(|S^{+}|=n-1\\), covering all cells of \\(U^{+}\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2. Lower‑triangular part\n\nBy symmetry, consider the **sub‑diagonal** cells  \n\n\\[\nS^{-}=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\subseteq U^{-}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle inside \\(U^{-}\\) must satisfy \\(\\displaystyle a>d\\); an argument completely analogous to the one above shows that such a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S^{-}\\).  \nSince \\(|S^{-}|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(U^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3. Total lower bound\n\nThe two parts \\(U^{+}\\) and \\(U^{-}\\) are disjoint, so the