 ,S_n\\subseteq[t]\\) all of the same size \\(\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor\\); such a family is an antichain and exists because \\(\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}\\ge n\\).  \n\n   For each \\(i\\) let \\(\\min(S_i)\\) denote the smallest element of \\(S_i\\).  \n   Define the tiles  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k = R_k\\times C_k\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,t)\n   \\]\n   with  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\{\\,i\\mid \\min(S_i)=k\\,\\},\\qquad   \n   C_k=\\{\\,j\\mid k\\notin S_j\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   *Why this works.*  \n   - Because \\(k\\in S_i\\) for every \\(i\\in R_k\\) while \\(k\\notin S_j\\) for every \\(j\\in C_k\\), we have \\(R_k\\cap C_k=\\varnothing\\); thus each \\(T_k\\) is a legitimate rectangle (no uncovered square lies inside it).  \n   - Take any ordered pair \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\). Since the family \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) is an antichain, the set \\(S_i\\setminus S_j\\) is non‑empty. Let  \n\n     \\[\n     k=\\min\\bigl(S_i\\setminus S_j\\bigr).\n     \\]\n\n     By definition \\(k\\in S_i\\) and \\(k\\notin S_j\\); moreover, because \\(k\\) is the smallest element of \\(S_i\\setminus S_j\\), no element smaller than \\(k\\) belongs to \\(S_i\\). Hence \\(\\min(S_i)=k\\), i.e. \\(i\\in R_k\\), and \\(j\\in C_k\\). Thus \\((i,j)\\in T_k\\).  \n\n   - Uniqueness: if \\((i,j)\\) belonged also to some \\(T_{k'}\\) with \\(k'\\neq k\\), then we would have \\(\\min(S_i)=k'=k\\), a contradiction. Hence each ordered pair appears in exactly one tile.\n\n   Therefore the \\(t\\) tiles \\(\\{T_k\\}_{k=1}^t\\) partition all off‑diagonal squares, providing a tiling with exactly \\(t\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Evaluation for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   Compute the smallest \\(t\\) with \\(\\binom{t}{\\lfloor t/2\\rfloor}\\ge2025\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\binom{13}{6}&=1716<2025,\\\\\n   \\binom{14}{7}&=3432\\ge2025.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the minimal feasible value is \\(t=14\\).\n\n   By the lower‑bound argument no tiling with fewer than 14 tiles exists, and the construction above shows that 14 tiles are sufficient.\n\n   Consequently the minimum number of rectangular tiles required is \\(\\boxed{14}\\)."
