inimize number of tiles. The solution claims we need at least 2025 tiles, and we can achieve 2025 tiles with a construction where each tile covers a single row (i) and all columns except column i (i.e., a row segment covering entire row except the diagonal square). But wait, a tile must be a rectangle. The construction uses R_i = {i}, C_i = [n] \\ {i}. That is a rectangle covering row i (i.e., a 1x(n-1) rectangle). Indeed, a rectangle covering row i, columns 1..i-1 and i+1..n (i.e., the entire row except column i). However, a rectangle must be contiguous: the columns must be a consecutive interval. The set [n] \\ {i} is not a contiguous interval; it's two intervals: columns 1..i-1 and i+1..n. That's not a single rectangle. So the construction is invalid: you cannot cover a row with a rectangular tile that has a hole (the uncovered square). A rectangle covering row i and all columns except column i would be two separate rectangles (or one rectangle that includes the uncovered square). But the tile cannot include uncovered squares (since each unit square is covered at most once, but uncovered squares must be uncovered). So the tile cannot cover the uncovered diagonal square. So the tile must be a rectangle that excludes that square. But a rectangle that excludes a single interior point is impossible if the rectangle is axis-aligned and includes the entire row except one column: you would need two separate rectangles (one left of the uncovered square, one right). So the construction fails to be a rectangle.\n\nThus the solution's construction is flawed. However, the lower bound argument might be correct? Let's examine the lower bound: they model each tile as a cut (R_k, C_k) where R_k ∩ C_k = ∅. They treat each tile as covering all pairs (i, j) where i ∈ R_k, j ∈ C_k. This corresponds to a rectangle that includes all rows in R_k and all columns in C_k. But a rectangle is defined by a contiguous set of rows and columns: i.e., R_k must be a set of consecutive rows, C_k a