he same as the right side tile for L_{j-1}. So we could have a chain of tiles where each tile serves as right side for one line and left side for the next line. That would reduce the total distinct tiles to n (or n-1?). Let's think: Suppose we have a set of tiles each of width 1, covering each column except uncovered squares, arranged such that each tile's right side is on L_j and left side on L_{j-1}. Then for each interior line L_j, the right side tile is tile j, left side tile is tile j+1. So each line has two distinct tiles, but overall there are n tiles (including maybe one extra). However, can such a set of width-1 tiles satisfy the row constraints? Let's try to design a tiling where each tile is a vertical strip of width 1 covering all rows except the uncovered square in that column. However, each column's uncovered square is at some row r(j). The vertical strip cannot include that square, so it must be split into two vertical strips: one above, one below. So a single width-1 tile cannot cover both above and below the uncovered square. So we cannot have a single width-1 tile for each column unless the uncovered square is at top or bottom. But we can choose uncovered squares to be at top row for all columns except maybe one? But each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So we cannot have all uncovered squares at top row; each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So we need a permutation sigma. If we choose sigma such that sigma(i) = i (diagonal), each column's uncovered square is at row i = column index. So each column's uncovered square is not at top or bottom except first and last. So each column needs two vertical tiles (above and below). So we need at least 2(n-1) vertical tiles? Actually we could use horizontal tiles to cover some of those squares. The construction uses vertical tiles for below diagonal and horizontal tiles for above diagonal. So each interior line L_j is the boundary between column j and j+1. The tile with right side on L_j is vertical tile V_j (covering rows > j). The tile with left side on L_j is horizontal tile H_j (covering columns > j). So each line uses distinct tiles. So total distinct tiles = 2(n-1). So the construction matches the lower bound.\n\nBut could there be a different permutation sigma that reduces the number of tiles? Let's explore possibilities. The lower bound argument is independent of sigma: For any tiling with any permutation sigma, the claim about each interior line holds. So the lower bound is universal. However, the claim about distinctness across lines may still hold: For each interior line L_j, there are two distinct tiles, T_right_j and T_left_j. Could it be that T_right_j = T_left_{j+1}? That would mean a tile has its right side on L_j and its left side on L_{j+1}. That would be a tile of width 1 covering column j+1? Wait, left side on L_{j+1} means the tile occupies columns j+2..something? Actually left side on L_{j+1} means the tile's left side is between columns j+1 and j+2, so the tile occupies columns j+2..something. Right side on L_j means tile's right side is between columns j and j+1. So the tile would have right side left of its left side, impossible. So a tile cannot be right side on L_j and left side on L_{j+1} because that would require its left side to be to the right of its right side. So T_right_j cannot equal T_left_{j+1}. However, T_left_j could equal T_right_{j-1}? Let's check: T_left_j has left side on L_j, right side somewhere to the right (maybe L_k). T_right_{j-1} has right side on L_{j-1}, left side somewhere left (maybe L_m). For them to be the same tile, we would need left side = L_j and right side = L_{j-1}, which again impossible because left side must be left of right side. So a tile cannot serve as left side for L_j and right side for L_{j-1}. So a tile cannot be counted for two lines in opposite roles. However, a tile could be right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1}? Let's check: Right side on L_j means its right side is at L_j, left side somewhere left (maybe L_{j-1} or earlier). If its left side is at L_{j-1}, then the tile's width is one column (column j). So it would be right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1}. So T_right_j could equal T_left_{j-1} (if tile width 1). That would mean a width-1 tile is counted as right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1}. So T_right_j = T_left_{j-1} is possible. Similarly, T_left_j could equal T_right_{j+1} (if width 1). So a tile can be counted for two adjacent lines, but in opposite roles: as right side for one line and left side for the previous line. So the total distinct tiles could be less than 2(n-1) if many width-1 tiles exist.\n\nNow, does the lower bound argument guarantee that each line's right side tile is distinct from the left side tile of the previous line? It doesn't. So the bound may be overcounting. However, we need to check if it's possible to have many width-1 tiles while satisfying the row constraints. Let's explore a potential tiling with fewer tiles.\n\nGoal: minimize number of tiles. Each tile is a rectangle. Tiles cannot overlap. Each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So each row has n-1 covered squares. Similarly each column has n-1 covered squares.\n\nWe can think of covering the grid with rectangles that avoid the uncovered squares. The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix (one per row and column). The problem reduces to covering the complement of a permutation matrix with axis-aligned rectangles that are disjoint.\n\nThis is reminiscent of covering a board with rectangles avoiding a set of points (the permutation). The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be 2n-2. Actually this is a known combinatorial geometry problem: covering the cells of an n x n board with rectangles that avoid a permutation of n points (i.e., a rook placement). The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all other cells is 2n-2. This is known as the \"grid covering problem\" or \"covering the board with rectangles avoiding a permutation\". The lower bound is often proven by counting arguments similar to the one used here. So the answer is indeed 2n-2. So the solution's bound is correct.\n\nBut we need to verify if the lower bound proof is rigorous. The typical proof uses the fact that each interior vertical line must intersect at least two rectangles, one on each side of the uncovered squares. However, they also need to argue that each rectangle can intersect at most two interior vertical lines (or something) to get the bound. Let's recall known results: For a permutation matrix, the complement can be partitioned into monotone rectangles (staircase shape). The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile the complement is 2n-2. The typical construction uses the \"staircase\" shape: vertical strips below diagonal and horizontal strips above diagonal. The lower bound uses the fact that each interior vertical line must be a boundary of at least two rectangles, and each rectangle can be a boundary for at most two interior vertical lines (since a rectangle has two vertical sides). So total number of rectangles >= number of interior vertical lines * 2 / 2 = n-1? Wait, each rectangle has two vertical sides, each interior vertical line can be a side of at most one rectangle? Actually each interior vertical line can be a side of at most one rectangle on each side (left side of one rectangle, right side of another). So each rectangle contributes at most two interior vertical lines (its left side and right side). So total distinct rectangles >= (2*(n-1))/2 = n-1? That would give n-1, not 2n-2. So we need a stronger argument.\n\nLet's think: The complement of a permutation matrix is a union of two monotone regions: the region above the permutation (i<j) and below (i>j). Each region can be covered by n-1 rectangles (horizontal strips for above, vertical strips for below). So total 2n-2. The lower bound can be proven by considering both vertical and horizontal lines: each interior vertical line must be a side of at least two rectangles, and each interior horizontal line must be a side of at least two rectangles. Since each rectangle has at most one vertical side on an interior line? Actually each rectangle has two vertical sides, but they could be interior lines or boundary lines. So each rectangle can be counted at most twice in vertical lines and at most twice in horizontal lines. So total rectangles >= max(2(n-1)/2, 2(n-1)/2) = n-1? That doesn't give 2n-2. So need a more careful counting: each rectangle must have at least one vertical side on an interior line or one horizontal side on an interior line? Not sure.\n\nLet's examine the solution's lower bound: They claim each interior vertical line yields two distinct tiles, and thus at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles. But as we argued, a tile could be counted for two lines (as right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1}). However, note that the tile with right side on L_j cannot have its left side on L_{j-1} if it also contains a square that is covered and not containing any uncovered squares. Could that happen? Let's examine the tile T_right_j: It covers the square (r(j+1), j). This tile cannot extend into column j+1. But could it extend leftwards beyond column j? Yes, it could extend leftwards to some column a <= j-1, as long as it doesn't include any uncovered squares. The uncovered square in column a is at (r(a), a). The tile covering (r(j+1), j) might also cover squares in column a. If it extends leftwards, it might include uncovered squares in those columns, which is not allowed. So the tile's left side must be at a column where there is no uncovered square in any of the columns it covers. But there is an uncovered square in each column. So if the tile extends leftwards beyond column j, it would include the uncovered square in that column (if it covers the row r(j+1)). However, the tile might not cover that row. For column a < j, the tile might not cover row r(j+1) if r(j+1) is not in the vertical range of the tile. So it's possible to have a tile that extends leftwards but only covers rows that avoid uncovered squares in those columns.\n\nThus a tile could be wide, covering multiple columns, but only a specific row range that avoids uncovered squares. For example, consider the tile covering row r(j+1) only (height 1) across columns a..j. This tile would have right side on L_j, left side on L_{a-1}. It would avoid uncovered squares in those columns because the uncovered squares are at rows r(a), r(a+1), ..., r(j+1). Since the tile is only at row r(j+1), it will intersect uncovered squares only if any of those columns have uncovered square at that same row. That would happen if sigma(i) = i for some i? Actually uncovered square in column a is at row r(a). If r(a) = r(j+1) for some a < j+1, then the tile would include that uncovered square, which is not allowed. But if the permutation sigma is such that r(j+1) is not equal to any r(a) for a < j+1, i.e., the row r(j+1) is not used as uncovered square in any earlier column, then the tile can extend leftwards across those columns without hitting uncovered squares. However, the permutation is a bijection, so each row appears exactly once as an uncovered square. So r(j+1) appears as uncovered square in column j+1 only. So for a < j+1, r(a) != r(j+1). So the tile covering row r(j+1) across columns a..j does not contain any uncovered squares. So it's allowed.\n\nThus the tile T_right_j could be a horizontal tile covering row r(j+1) across multiple columns leftwards, not just column j. Similarly, T_left_j could be a horizontal tile covering row r(j) across columns j+1..some. So the tile could be counted for multiple lines.\n\nNow, the lower bound argument only says there are at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles. But as we see, a tile could be counted for two lines, reducing the distinct count. However, can a single tile be counted for more than two lines? Possibly, if