in that column. So each column would need two vertical tiles (above and below) unless the uncovered square is at top or bottom. So width-1 vertical tile covering column j cannot cover both above and below the uncovered square; it can only cover one side. So we would need at least one tile per column for each side of the uncovered square. So we need at least 2(n-1) vertical tiles if uncovered squares are not at top or bottom. So the lower bound may be correct because each column's uncovered square forces at least one tile on each side of it (above and below). However, the solution's argument uses vertical lines, not columns. But maybe the vertical line argument implicitly accounts for both above and below because each interior vertical line yields two tiles, one on each side of the uncovered square in the column to the right and left. But does that guarantee that each column's uncovered square is covered by two tiles? Let's examine.\n\nTake column j. The uncovered square is at (r(j), j). The vertical line L_{j-1} (between columns j-1 and j) yields a tile with right side on L_{j-1} (covering square (r(j), j-1)). That tile cannot extend into column j, so it's left of column j. That tile is not covering column j. The vertical line L_j yields a tile with left side on L_j (covering square (r(j), j+1)). That tile cannot extend into column j, so it's right of column j. So the vertical line argument does not guarantee any tile covering column j. So we need to consider horizontal lines for covering column j. The solution's lower bound only uses vertical lines, but maybe the argument is enough because each interior vertical line yields two tiles, and there are n-1 lines, so total tiles >= 2(n-1). But as we argued, a tile could be counted for two lines, reducing distinct count. However, can a tile be counted for two lines while still covering all squares? Let's examine the possibility of a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles that still satisfies the constraints. Could we design a