use the tile is a rectangle; but (r(j+1), j+1) is uncovered, so tile cannot include it. So tile must stop at column j. So its right side is on L_j. Similarly for left side using uncovered square in column j.\n\nThus each interior vertical line has at least two distinct tiles. So lower bound is 2(n-1). That seems valid.\n\nConstruction: Uncovered squares on diagonal. Then vertical tiles V_j covering column j rows j+1..n. Horizontal tiles H_i covering row i columns i+1..n. These are indeed disjoint: V_j occupies column j rows > j; H_i occupies row i columns > i. For any pair (i,j) with i>j, it's in V_j; if i<j, it's in H_i; if i=j, it's uncovered. So they cover all off-diagonal squares exactly once. So number of tiles = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2.\n\nThus the answer 4048 is correct.\n\nNow we need to apply the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nLevel 1: Check answer correctness. The final answer is 4048. Is that correct? We think yes. So Level 1: CORRECT.\n\nLevel 2: Check reasoning validity. The solution uses case analysis, counting argument, construction. The lower bound uses a lemma about interior vertical lines. That's a valid method. The construction uses explicit specification: vertical tiles V_j defined as set of squares (i,j) for i=j+1..n, and horizontal tiles H_i defined similarly. That is concrete detail (Category C). So the reasoning uses valid methods. No invalid method like trial-and-error. So Level 2: VALID.\n\nBut we need to check the \"Critical Level 2 Enhancement - Justification Completeness (MANDATORY GATE CHECK)\" about construction claims: The solution claims a construction exists and provides explicit definitions of V_j and H_i. Those are concrete details: they specify exactly which squares each tile covers. So they belong to Category C (VALID). So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. Look for justification gaps or critical errors.\n\nPotential gaps: The solution's lower bound argument might be missing justification that the two tiles are dis