ight double count some tiles if they are width 1. But the argument also says the two tiles for a given line are distinct: T_right_j and T_left_j are distinct because a rectangle cannot have both left and right side on same line. However, the counting across lines: The tile that is right side for L_j could be left side for L_{j+1} if it is width 1. Then that tile is counted as a right side tile for L_j and also as a left side tile for L_{j+1}. So the total number of distinct tiles might be less than 2(n-1). The argument that there are at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles assumes that the tiles for different lines are distinct across lines. But they didn't prove that. They only proved for each line there are two distinct tiles, but they didn't prove that the tile for line j is not the same as a tile for line j+1 (or any other line). So the lower bound might be overcounting. However, the construction uses vertical and horizontal tiles each of width 1 or height 1, but they are not width 1 for vertical tiles: vertical tiles V_j have width 1 (since they occupy a single column). So each vertical tile V_j has left side on L_{j-1} and right side on L_j. So V_j is left side tile for L_{j-1} and right side tile for L_j. Similarly, horizontal tiles H_i have height 1, they occupy a single row, so they have top side on some horizontal line and bottom side on another. But for vertical lines, horizontal tiles have left side on L_i and right side on L_n? Actually horizontal tile H_i occupies row i, columns i+1..n. So its left side is at L_i (between columns i and i+1), and its right side is at L_n (the rightmost boundary). So H_i is left side tile for L_i, not right side for any interior line. So each interior line L_j (j from 1 to n-1) has a right side tile V_j (vertical tile) and a left side tile H_j (horizontal tile). So each tile appears exactly twice: V_j appears as right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1} (except V_1 has left side on L_0 which is left boundary). So the lower bound counting each line's two tiles yields 2(n-1) tile occurrences, but each vertical tile appears twice (except V_1 maybe appears only once as right side for L_1, left side for L_0 which is not counted). Similarly each horizontal tile appears once as left side for L_i, and maybe also as right side for L_n? Actually horizontal tile H_i's right side is at L_n (the rightmost boundary), which is not an interior line. So H_i appears only once in the count for L_i. So total distinct tiles in the construction: vertical tiles V_j for j=1..n-1 (n-1 tiles) + horizontal tiles H_i for i=1..n-1 (n-1 tiles) = 2(n-1). So the construction matches the bound. However, the lower bound argument might be flawed if there could be a tiling with fewer than 2(n-1) tiles using some width-1 tiles that serve as both left and right side for adjacent lines, reducing total count. Let's examine if it's possible to have a tiling with fewer than 2(n-1) tiles.\n\nThe lower bound argument: For each interior vertical line L_j, there exist two distinct tiles, one with right side on L_j and one with left side on L_j. So we have at least 2(n-1) tile occurrences. But we need to argue that these occurrences correspond to distinct tiles across all lines. The solution says \"Since the claim holds for each of the n-1 interior vertical lines, any tiling T must contain at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles\". This is a leap: they assume that the tiles for different lines are distinct. However, as we noted, a tile of width 1 could be counted for two lines: as right side for L_j and left side for L_{j+1}. So the claim that there are at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles is not automatically valid. They need to argue that a tile cannot be counted for two lines. Let's examine if a tile can have both its left side on L_j and its right side on L_{j+1} (i.e., width 1). That tile would be counted as left side tile for L_j and right side tile for L_{j+1}. So the tile appears twice in the count. So the number of distinct tiles could be as low as (2(n-1) - (n-2)) = n+? Actually if each interior line's right side tile is the same as the left side tile of the next line, we could reduce the count. Let's consider a tiling where all tiles are width 1 vertical strips covering each column except maybe some columns? But each column must have exactly one uncovered square, so each column must have at least one tile covering the squares above or below the uncovered square. The vertical strip covering column j could be split into two vertical tiles: one above the uncovered square and one below. But the solution's lower bound uses uncovered squares to guarantee a tile with right side on L_j and a tile with left side on L_j. Let's see if a single tile can satisfy both conditions for L_j: It would need to have both its right side on L_j and its left side on L_j, which is impossible (zero width). So for each line L_j, we need two distinct tiles. However, the same tile could serve as the right side tile for L_j and also as the left side tile for L_{j+1} if it has width 1. So the tile appears in two lines' counts, but for each line we still have two distinct tiles. So the total distinct tiles could be less than 2(n-1). The solution incorrectly assumes distinctness across lines.\n\nThus the lower bound argument may be flawed. Let's examine if we can construct a tiling with fewer than 2(n-1) tiles. Suppose we try to use vertical tiles of width 1 for each column, each covering all squares except the uncovered one. That would be n tiles (one per column). But each column has exactly one uncovered square, so we can have a vertical tile covering all squares above the uncovered square and another covering all squares below? Actually a vertical tile covering the whole column except the uncovered square cannot be a rectangle because the uncovered square is a hole. So we need to split the column into two vertical tiles: one above the uncovered square, one below. So each column would need at least two tiles (unless the uncovered square is at the top or bottom edge). But the uncovered square could be at the top row (i=1) or bottom row (i=n). If uncovered square is at top row of a column, then we can have a vertical tile covering rows 2..n for that column (one tile). Similarly if uncovered square is at bottom row, we can have a tile covering rows 1..n-1. So each column could need either one or two vertical tiles depending on the position of the uncovered square. However, we also have rows constraints. So we need to consider both rows and columns.\n\nThe solution's construction uses diagonal uncovered squares, requiring each column's uncovered square is at row i = column index. So each column's uncovered square is not at top or bottom (except maybe first and last). For column 1, uncovered square at (1,1) is at top row, so we can have a vertical tile covering rows 2..n for column 1 (that's V_1). For column n, uncovered square at (n,n) is at bottom row, but we don't have V_n because j runs to n-1. So column n's uncovered square is at bottom row, but we have horizontal tile H_n? Actually H_i defined for i=1..n-1, so row n's uncovered square at (n,n) is at bottom column, but we don't need a tile for row n because it's uncovered. The rest of row n (columns 1..n-1) are covered by vertical tiles V_j for j=1..n-1 (since V_j covers column j rows > j). For row n, all columns j < n have V_j covering row n (since i=n > j). So row n is covered by vertical tiles. So the construction uses exactly 2(n-1) tiles.\n\nBut could there be a tiling with fewer tiles? Let's try to think: The lower bound argument might be salvageable if we can prove that each tile can be counted at most once across all lines. But we saw a width-1 tile can be counted twice. However, does the existence of a width-1 tile conflict with the condition that each row also has exactly one uncovered square? Let's examine: Suppose we have a width-1 vertical tile covering column j rows a..b. It has left side on L_{j-1} and right side on L_j. So it's the right side tile for L_j. It could also be left side tile for L_{j-1} if its left side is on L_{j-2}? Wait, no, left side is on L_{j-1} because it's a vertical tile occupying column j, so left side is on L_{j-1}, right side on L_j. So it's left side tile for L_{j-1} and right side tile for L_j. So it is counted for both L_{j-1} and L_j. So the lower bound argument would double count that tile. So the bound might be 2(n-1) - (number of width-1 tiles). But perhaps width-1 tiles cannot exist in a minimal tiling because of row constraints? Let's examine.\n\nIf we have a width-1 vertical tile covering column j, then it occupies all rows except maybe the uncovered square in column j. But the uncovered square in column j is at row r(j). The vertical tile cannot include that square. So the vertical tile must be split into two vertical tiles: one above the uncovered square, one below, unless the uncovered square is at top or bottom. So a width-1 vertical tile cannot cover both above and below the uncovered square because it would be a rectangle that includes the uncovered square. So a width-1 vertical tile can only cover a contiguous block of rows in column j that does not include the uncovered square. So it could be either the block above the uncovered square (if uncovered square not at top) or below (if not at bottom). So we could have a vertical tile covering rows 1..r(j)-1 in column j (if r(j)>1) and another covering rows r(j)+1..n (if r(j)<n). So each column may need up to two vertical tiles. So width-1 vertical tiles exist but each column may need up to two such tiles. However, the lower bound argument uses the uncovered square in column j+1 to guarantee a tile with right side on L_j. That tile could be the vertical tile covering rows above the uncovered square in column j+1? Wait, the tile covering square (r(j+1), j) is the tile covering the square left of the uncovered square in column j+1. That tile could be a horizontal tile that extends across multiple columns? Let's examine: The square (r(j+1), j) is in row r(j+1), column j. The tile covering it cannot extend into column j+1 because of uncovered square at (r(j+1), j+1). So the tile's right side is on L_j. But that tile could be a horizontal tile covering row r(j+1) columns some range that ends at column j. So it could be a horizontal tile of height 1 covering row r(j+1) columns a..j. That tile would have right side on L_j, left side on L_{a-1} (some interior line). So that tile could also be left side tile for L_{a-1}. So again, a tile can be counted for two lines.\n\nThus the lower bound argument may not guarantee distinctness across lines. However, the solution asserts \"any tiling must contain at least 2(n-1) distinct tiles\". This is a claim that may be false if some tile can serve as the right side for one line and left side for the adjacent line, reducing the total distinct count. But does that reduce the total distinct count enough to go below 2(n-1)? Let's try to see if we can achieve a tiling with n tiles (or less than 2n-2). Let's attempt to construct a tiling with n tiles: For each column j, we could have a vertical tile covering all squares except the uncovered one, but we need to split into two vertical tiles if uncovered square not at top or bottom. So each column may need up to 2 tiles. So total tiles could be up to 2n. But maybe we can use horizontal tiles to reduce count. The lower bound seems plausible but we need to verify if it's indeed minimal.\n\nLet's try to see if we can find a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 tiles for n=2025. Could we use a combination of vertical and horizontal tiles that share lines? For each interior vertical line L_j, we need a tile with right side on L_j and a tile with left side on L_j. If we have a tile that is right side for L_j and left side for L_{j+1}, then that tile would be counted for both lines. But we still need a left side tile for L_j (different from the right side tile for L_j). That left side tile could be the same as the right side tile for L_{j-1}. So we could have a chain of tiles where each tile serves as right side for one line and left side for the next line. That would reduce the total distinct tiles to n (or n-1?). Let's think: Suppose we have a set of tiles each of width 1, covering each column except uncovered squares, arranged such that each tile's right side is on L_j and left side on L_{j-1}. Then for each interior line L_j, the right side tile is tile j, left side tile is tile j+1. So each line has two distinct tiles, but overall there are n tiles (including maybe one extra). However, can such a set of width-1 tiles satisfy the row constraints? Let's try to design a tiling where each tile is a vertical strip of width 1 covering all rows except the uncovered square in that column. However, each column's uncovered square is at some row r(j). The vertical strip cannot include that square, so it must be split into two vertical strips: one above, one below. So a single width-1 tile cannot cover both above and below the uncovered square. So we cannot have a single width-1 tile for each column unless the uncovered square is at top or bottom. But we can choose uncovered squares to be at top row for all columns except maybe one? But each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So we cannot have all uncovered squares at top row; each row must have exactly one uncovered square. So we need a permutation sigma. If we choose sigma such that sigma(i) = i (diagonal), each column's uncovered square is at row i = column index. So each column's uncovered square is not at top or bottom except first and last. So each column needs two vertical tiles (above and below). So we need at least 2(n-1) vertical tiles? Actually we could use horizontal tiles to cover some of those squares. The construction uses vertical tiles for below diagonal and horizontal tiles for above diagonal. So each interior line L_j is the boundary between column j and j+1. The tile with right side on L_j is vertical tile V_j (covering rows > j). The tile with left side on L_j is horizontal tile H_j (covering columns > j). So each line uses distinct tiles. So total distinct tiles = 2(n-1). So the construction matches the lower bound.\n\nBut could there be a different permutation sigma that reduces the number of tiles? Let's explore possibilities. The lower bound argument is independent of sigma: For any tiling with any permutation sigma, the claim about each interior line holds. So the lower bound is universal. However, the claim about distinctness across lines may still hold: For each interior line L_j, there are two distinct tiles, T_right_j and T_left_j. Could it be that T_right_j = T_left_{j+1}? That would mean a tile has its right side on L_j and its left side on L_{j+1}. That would be a tile of width 1 covering column j+1? Wait, left side on L_{j+1} means the tile occupies columns j+2..something? Actually left side on L_{j+1} means the tile's left side is between columns j+1 and j+2, so the tile occupies columns j+2..something. Right side on L_j means tile's right side is between columns j and j+1. So the tile would have right side left of its left side, impossible. So a tile cannot be right side on L_j and left side on L_{j+1} because that would require its left side to be to the right of its right side. So T_right_j cannot equal T_left_{j+1}. However, T_left_j could equal T_right_{j-1}? Let's check: T_left_j has left side on L_j, right side somewhere to the right (maybe L_k). T_right_{j-1} has right side on L_{j-1}, left side somewhere left (maybe L_m). For them to be the same tile, we would need left side = L_j and right side = L_{j-1}, which again impossible because left side must be left of right side. So a tile cannot serve as left side for L_j and right side for L_{j-1}. So a tile cannot be counted for two lines in opposite roles. However, a tile could be right side for L_j and left side for L_{j-1}? Let's check: Right side on L_j means its right side is