false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  Let \\(n=2025\\).  In each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by \\(p(i)\\).  Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n   \\[\n   p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad i\\mapsto p(i)\n   \\]\n\n   is a permutation.  Write \\(q=p^{-1}\\); thus the uncovered square in column \\(j\\) lies in row \\(q(j)\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound (construction).**  \n   Row and column permutations do not affect the number of tiles: relabelling rows and columns simply relabels every rectangle.  Hence we may assume \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) (the uncovered squares are the main diagonal).  \n   - For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile that covers the whole part of row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal.  \n   - For each \\(j=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile that covers the whole part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  \n   The families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square except the diagonal, using exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.  Undoing the row/column permutations yields a tiling of the original board with the same number of tiles.\n\n3. **Lower bound (induction).**  \n   Let \\(T(n)\\) denote the minimum number of tiles for an \\(n\\times n\\) board with the required property.  \n\n   *Base case.*  \\(T(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).  \n\n   *Inductive step.*  Assume \\(T(k)\\ge 2k-2\\) for all \\(k<n\\).  \n   Consider an \\(n\\times n\\) board and let \\((r,c)\\) be an uncovered square such that at least one of \\(r<n\\) or \\(c<n\\) holds (if the only uncovered square with \\(r=n\\) is at \\((n,n)\\) we treat the symmetric case).  \n\n   Remove row \\(r\\) and column \\(c\\).  The remaining \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) board still has exactly one uncovered square in each of its rows and columns, because column \\(c\\) contained the uncovered square only in row \\(r\\) and row \\(r\\) contained the uncovered square only in column \\(c\\).  Hence any tiling of the original board induces a tiling of the reduced board, so the original board uses at least \\(T(n-1)\\) tiles on that part.\n\n   The squares that have been removed consist of the \\(n-1\\) squares of row \\(r\\) to the left of column \\(c\\) and the \\(n-1\\) squares of column \\(c\\) above row \\(r\\); they form a “cross’’ whose two arms intersect only at the uncovered square \\((r,c)\\).  A rectangle that contains a square of the horizontal arm cannot contain a square of the vertical arm, for otherwise the rectangle would also contain the intersection \\((r,c)\\), which is forbidden.  Consequently at least **two** tiles are required to cover this cross (one horizontal \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) tile and one vertical \\((n-1)\\times1\\) tile suffice).  \n\n   Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   T(n)\\ge T(n-1)+2.\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis \\(T(n-1)\\ge 2(n-1)-2=2n-4\\); adding the two extra tiles gives  \n\n   \\[\n   T(n)\\ge (2n-4)+2=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds also when the uncovered square with \\(r=n\\) lies in column \\(n\\) (the same argument with the last row and last column).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(T(n)\\ge 2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles show that the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²