ma(i))\\) of some row \\(i\\) that it meets.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for the left region  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_c=\\sigma^{-1}(c+1),\n\\]\n\nthe unique row whose uncovered column equals \\(c+1\\).  \nBecause \\(\\sigma(r_c)=c+1>c\\), the cell \\((r_c,c)\\) belongs to \\(L\\).  \nMoreover \\((r_c,c)\\) is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(r_c\\) that lies in \\(L\\) (j<\\sigma(r_c))\\).\n\nLet \\(R\\) be any rectangle (tile) that contains \\((r_c,c)\\).  \nSince a rectangle’s column interval is contiguous, the presence of the missing cell \\((r_c,c+1)\\) forces the column interval of \\(R\\) to be contained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\); otherwise column \\(c+1\\) would be included, contradicting the fact that \\((r_c,c+1)\\) is uncovered.  \nThus the rightmost column of \\(R\\) is exactly \\(c\\).\n\nConsequently **different values of \\(c\\) require different rectangles**: a single rectangle cannot have two distinct rightmost columns.  \nBecause there are \\(n-1\\) values of \\(c\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for the right region  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_c=\\sigma^{-1}(c-1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(\\sigma(r_c)=c-1<c\\), so the cell \\((r_c,c)\\) lies in \\(R\\) and is the **leftmost** cell of its row in \\(R\\).  \nIf a rectangle contains \\((r_c,c)\\), it cannot contain column \\(c-1\\) (the missing cell \\((r_c,c-1)\\) is uncovered), hence its leftmost column is \\(c\\).  \nThus each rectangle can serve as the leftmost column for at most one value of \\(c\\), and we again need at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Total lower bound  \n\nAny tiling of the whole board consists of a collection of left‑type rectangles covering \\(L\\) and right‑type rectangles covering \\(R\\).  \nFrom Sections 3 and 4 we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the **identity permutation** \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left‑type tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(i,c):i=c+1,c+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(V_c\\) is a rectangle of size \\((n-c)\\times1\\); it lies entirely in \\(L\\) because for every row \\(i\\ge c+1\\) we have \\(c<\\sigma(i)=i\\).\n\n*Right‑type tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(r\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_r=\\{(r,j):j=r+1,r+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H_r\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(n-r)\\); it lies entirely in \\(R\\) because for the fixed row \\(r\\) we have \\(j>\\sigma(r)=r\\).\n\nThe \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{V_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_r\\}_{r=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint: a vertical tile uses a single column, a horizontal tile uses a single row, and the only possible intersection would be a diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\), which belongs to neither family.  \n\nEvery non‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) is covered exactly once:\n\n* If \\(j<i\\) then \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,i-1\\}\\) and the cell belongs to the vertical tile \\(V_j\\).\n* If \\(j>i\\) then \\(j\\in\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and the cell belongs to the horizontal tile \\(H_i\\).\n\nThe diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) are uncovered, providing exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\n\nThus we have a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (1) and the explicit construction both give the same number of tiles.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
