                "reasoning_content": "We have a problem: \"Consider a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nSolution: They propose a lower bound of 2n-2 tiles (n=2025) and a construction achieving 2n-2 tiles, thus answer 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate using the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, check answer correctness: The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem. We need to find minimal number of tiles such that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. Tiles are rectangles covering unit squares, no overlap (each unit square covered at most once). So we need to cover all squares except exactly one per row and column. So there are n rows and n columns, each row has exactly one uncovered square, each column has exactly one uncovered square. So uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: exactly one uncovered per row and column. So there are n uncovered squares, each at distinct row and column. The rest of the n^2 - n squares must be covered by tiles, each tile is a rectangle covering a set of unit squares, no overlap.\n\nGoal: minimize number of tiles needed to cover all other squares. Equivalent to partition the set of covered squares into rectangles (tiles) such that each tile is axis-aligned and covers a contiguous rectangle of unit squares. The uncovered squares are \"holes\" that cannot be inside any tile; they must be left uncovered. So we need to tile the rest of the grid (the complement of the permutation matrix) with axis-aligned rectangles.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles needed to tile the complement of a permutation matrix.\n\nThe solution claims lower bound 2n-2 and constructs a tiling achieving that bound, using identity permutation (diagonal uncovered). They use vertical tiles covering all squares below diagonal in each column (except diagonal) and horizontal tiles covering all squares to the right of diagonal in each row (except diagonal). That yields n-1 vertical tiles (columns 1 to n-1) each covering squares below diagonal in that column, and n-1 horizontal tiles (rows 1 to n-1) each covering squares to the right of diagonal in that row. So total 2n-2 tiles. Indeed, they cover all non-diagonal squares exactly once: For any cell (i,j) with i>j, it's covered by vertical tile V_j (since column j, rows > j). For any cell (i,j) with i<j, it's covered by horizontal tile H_i (since row i, columns > i). The diagonal squares are uncovered. So this is a valid tiling.\n\nNow, is 2n-2 minimal? The solution provides a lower bound argument using \"critical cells\" L_c and R_c. They argue each interior column c (2 ≤ c ≤ n-1) forces at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column c (covering L_c) and one with leftmost column c (covering R_c). They argue a tile cannot have both leftmost and rightmost column c unless width 1, and a width-1 tile can contain at most one of L_c, R_c. So each interior column forces at least two distinct tiles. Border columns force at least one tile each. So total at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nWe need to verify the correctness of this lower bound argument. It seems plausible. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have uncovered squares at positions (i, σ(i)). For each column c, they define L_c = (σ^{-1}(c+1), c) which is the cell immediately left of the uncovered square in column c+1 (i.e., the uncovered square in column c+1 is at row σ^{-1}(c+1), column c+1). So L_c is the cell in the same row but column c. Similarly, R_c = (σ^{-1}(c-1), c) is the cell immediately right of the uncovered square in column c-1 (the uncovered square in column c-1 is at row σ^{-1}(c-1), column c-1). So R_c is the cell in the same row but column c.\n\nThus L_c is the rightmost covered cell in the row of the uncovered square at column c+1 (since it's the cell left of that uncovered square). Similarly, R_c is the leftmost covered cell in the row of the uncovered square at column c-1 (since it's the cell right of that uncovered square). So each column c (except border) has two critical cells: L_c and R_c. For border columns c=1 and c=n, only one of them exists: L_1 (leftmost column has uncovered square at column 2's row, so L_1 is left of that uncovered square) and R_n (rightmost column has uncovered square at column n-1's row, so R_n is right of that uncovered square). So total critical cells count = 2n-2.\n\nNow they argue each tile containing L_c must have rightmost column exactly c (since column c+1 cannot be part of tile because that column contains the uncovered square in that row). Similarly, each tile containing R_c must have leftmost column exactly c (since column c-1 cannot be part of tile). So each interior column c requires at least one tile with rightmost column c (covering L_c) and at least one tile with leftmost column c (covering R_c). Could the same tile satisfy both? That would require a tile whose leftmost column = rightmost column = c, i.e., width 1. A width-1 tile is a vertical strip covering only column c. They argue such a tile can contain at most one of L_c and R_c because if it contained both, then it would contain the uncovered square (σ^{-1}(c), c) which is not allowed. Actually, L_c is at column c, row σ^{-1}(c+1). R_c is at column c, row σ^{-1}(c-1). The uncovered square in column c is at row σ^{-1}(c), column c. So a vertical tile covering column c would cover a set of rows. It could possibly include both L_c and R_c if it includes rows σ^{-1}(c+1) and σ^{-1}(c-1). But if it includes the row σ^{-1}(c) (the uncovered square), that would be illegal. However, a vertical tile could be chosen to avoid that row, e.g., covering rows above σ^{-1}(c) but not including it, or rows below σ^{-1}(c) but not including it. But can it cover both L_c and R_c while avoiding the uncovered square? Let's think: L_c is in row σ^{-1}(c+1). R_c is in row σ^{-1}(c-1). The uncovered square is at row σ^{-1}(c). So the three rows are distinct (since σ is a permutation). The vertical tile would be a contiguous set of rows (since it's a rectangle). To contain both L_c and R_c, the tile would need to include rows from min(σ^{-1}(c-1), σ^{-1}(c+1)) to max(σ^{-1}(c-1), σ^{-1}(c+1)). That interval would contain σ^{-1}(c) unless σ^{-1}(c) lies outside that interval. But the three rows are distinct; we need to consider possible ordering. For example, suppose σ^{-1}(c-1) < σ^{-1}(c) < σ^{-1}(c+1). Then the interval covering both σ^{-1}(c-1) and σ^{-1}(c+1) includes σ^{-1}(c) in between, so tile would contain uncovered square. If σ^{-1}(c) is either less than both or greater than both, then the interval covering σ^{-1}(c-1) and σ^{-1}(c+1) would not include σ^{-1}(c). But is that possible? Since σ is a permutation, the ordering of σ^{-1} values is arbitrary. So it's possible that σ^{-1}(c) is not between σ^{-1}(c-1) and σ^{-1}(c+1). For instance, if σ^{-1}(c-1) = 5, σ^{-1}(c) = 2, σ^{-1}(c+1) = 8, then the interval covering rows 5 and 8 includes rows 5,6,7,8, which does not include row 2. So a vertical tile covering rows 5 through 8 would include L_c (row 8) and R_c (row 5) but not the uncovered square (row 2). However, the tile must be contiguous rows, but can we have a vertical tile that includes rows 5-8 but not row 2? Yes. So the claim that a width-1 tile cannot contain both L_c and R_c may be false. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe tile is a rectangle covering a set of unit squares. If it's width-1 (i.e., a vertical strip of column c), its rows must be a contiguous interval (since it's a rectangle). So if we want to include both rows σ^{-1}(c-1) and σ^{-1}(c+1), we need to include all rows between them inclusive. If σ^{-1}(c) lies between those two rows, then the tile would include the uncovered square at row σ^{-1}(c). If σ^{-1}(c) lies outside that interval, then the tile can include both L_c and R_c without covering the uncovered square. So the claim that a width-1 tile can contain at most one of L_c and R_c is not always true; it depends on the ordering of the rows.\n\nBut perhaps the solution's argument is that L_c and R_c are on opposite sides of the uncovered square in column c, i.e., one is left of uncovered square in column c+1 and the other is right of uncovered square in column c-1. However, they are not necessarily on opposite sides of the uncovered square in column c. Let's examine the geometry.\n\nWe have uncovered squares at positions (i, σ(i)). For column c, the uncovered square is at (σ^{-1}(c), c). L_c is at (σ^{-1}(c+1), c). R_c is at (σ^{-1}(c-1), c). So L_c and R_c are in the same column c but different rows: row of L_c is the row where the uncovered square in column c+1 sits; row of R_c is the row where the uncovered square in column c-1 sits. The uncovered square in column c is at row σ^{-1}(c). So we have three rows: σ^{-1}(c-1), σ^{-1}(c), σ^{-1}(c+1). Their relative ordering can be any permutation of three distinct numbers. So L_c and R_c could be both above the uncovered square, both below, or one above and one below. If both are on the same side (both above or both below), then a vertical tile covering that side could include both L_c and R_c without covering the uncovered square. So the argument that they must be covered by distinct tiles may be flawed.\n\nBut perhaps the solution's argument is that a tile containing L_c must have rightmost column c, but could have leftmost column less than c (i.e., extend leftwards). Similarly, a tile containing R_c must have leftmost column c, but could extend rightwards. So a tile could contain both L_c and R_c if it has leftmost column c and rightmost column c (i.e., width 1). But they argue width-1 tile can contain at most one of them because it cannot cross the uncovered square. However, as we argued, it could contain both if they are on the same side of the uncovered square. But is that possible given the definitions? Let's examine more concretely.\n\nTake a permutation σ. For column c, we have three rows: a = σ^{-1}(c-1), b = σ^{-1}(c), d = σ^{-1}(c+1). L_c is at (d, c). R_c is at (a, c). So L_c row = d, R_c row = a. The uncovered square row = b. So we have three distinct rows a, b, d. The tile covering column c can be any contiguous interval of rows that includes a and d but not b. This is possible if b is not between a and d. So if b < min(a,d) or b > max(a,d), then the interval [min(a,d), max(a,d)] does not contain b. But the tile must be contiguous rows; it could be exactly rows from min(a,d) to max(a,d). That includes both a and d, but not b. So a width-1 tile could contain both L_c and R_c.\n\nThus the lower bound argument might be invalid. However, perhaps there is an additional constraint: the tile must be a rectangle of covered squares, and cannot contain any uncovered squares. So the tile cannot contain any uncovered squares. So the tile covering column c must avoid the uncovered square at row b. But it also must avoid any other uncovered squares in other columns? Actually, any tile can contain uncovered squares in other columns? No, a tile cannot contain any uncovered squares at all, because uncovered squares are not covered. So any tile must avoid all uncovered squares. So a vertical tile in column c can only cover rows that do not contain any uncovered squares in any column. Wait, but a tile can be multiple columns wide. But if a tile includes column c, it cannot include any uncovered squares in any column that intersect its rectangle. So the tile must avoid all uncovered squares in its rectangle.\n\nThus the vertical tile in column c cannot include any row i such that there is an uncovered square in any column j that lies within the tile's column range. For a width-1 tile (only column c), the only uncovered squares that could be in that column are the one at row b. So the tile must avoid row b. But there are uncovered squares in other columns at rows a and d? Actually, a = σ^{-1}(c-1) is the row of the uncovered square in column c-1. That uncovered square is at column c-1, not column c. So a row is not a problem for a width-1 tile in column c, because the tile only covers column c. So the tile can include row a (since the uncovered square at row a is in column c-1, not in column c). Similarly, row d is the row of uncovered square in column c+1, which is at column c+1, not column c. So a width-1 tile can include rows a and d without covering any uncovered squares. So indeed a vertical tile can contain both L_c and R_c, as long as it does not include row b.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each interior column forces two distinct tiles is flawed. However, perhaps they have a more subtle argument: they claim that a tile containing L_c must have rightmost column exactly c, because column c+1 cannot be part of the tile (since it contains the uncovered square at row d). But the tile could also have leftmost column less than c (i.e., extend leftwards). Similarly, a tile containing R_c must have leftmost column exactly c, because column c-1 cannot be part of the tile. But a tile could also have rightmost column greater than c (i.e., extend rightwards). So a tile could contain both L_c and R_c if it has leftmost column = c and rightmost column = c (width 1). So they argue width-1 tile can contain at most one of L_c and R_c because it cannot contain the uncovered square at row b. But as we argued, it can contain both L_c and R_c if they are on the same side of row b. So the claim that width-1 tile can contain at most one is not universally true. However, maybe they also require that the tile must be \"maximal\" or something? No, they didn't require maximal.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be valid. But perhaps there is a known result that the minimum number of rectangles needed to tile the complement of a permutation matrix is indeed 2n-2. Let's think about known combinatorial geometry results: The complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., an n x n board with n rooks placed such that each row and column has exactly one rook) can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles. Is that minimal? I recall a known problem: \"Cover all cells of an n x n board except one in each row and column with the minimum number of rectangles.\" The answer is indeed 2n-2. I think I've seen a similar problem: \"Given an n x n board with exactly one cell in each row and column left uncovered, what is the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover the rest?\" The answer is 2n-2. The construction using the diagonal works and the lower bound uses a combinatorial argument about \"critical cells\". However, we need to verify if the lower bound argument is correct. There might be a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix has a rectangle covering number equal to 2n-2. This is known as the \"grid covering number\" or something. Let's search memory: There's a known problem: \"Find the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all cells of an n x n board except one in each row and column.\" The answer is 2n-2. The lower bound often uses the concept of \"critical cells\" as in the solution. The argument that each interior column forces at least two distinct rectangles is standard: For each interior column c, there are two cells adjacent to the uncovered cells: one left of the uncovered cell in column c+1, and one right of the uncovered cell in column c-1. These two cells cannot be covered by the same rectangle because any rectangle covering both would also cover the uncovered cell in column c (the one between them). However, as we argued, they might be on the same side of that uncovered cell, but maybe the geometry ensures they are on opposite sides? Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe uncovered squares are at positions (i, σ(i)). For column c, we have uncovered square at (σ^{-1}(c), c). The cell L_c is at (σ^{-1}(c+1), c). The cell R_c is at (σ^{-1}(c-1), c). Are these cells necessarily on opposite sides of the uncovered square in column c? Let's think: The uncovered square in column c+1 is at row σ^{-1}(c+1). The uncovered square in column c-1 is at row σ^{-1}(c-1). The uncovered square in column c is at row σ^{-1}(c). There is no guarantee about ordering. However, perhaps the definitions of L_c and R_c guarantee that L_c is to the left of the uncovered square in column c+1 (i.e., same row, column c) and R_c is to the right of the uncovered square in column c-1 (same row, column c). But both are in column c. So they are both in the same column c. So they are not left or right of the uncovered square in column c; they are above or below relative to rows.\n\nNow, the rectangle covering both L_c and R_c would be a vertical rectangle (since they share column c). For it to also contain the uncovered square at (σ^{-1}(c), c), that uncovered square would have to be between the rows of L_c and R_c. If the uncovered square's row is between them, then any vertical rectangle covering both L_c and R_c would also cover the uncovered square, which is not allowed. But if the uncovered square's row is not between them (i.e., it's either above both or below both), then a vertical rectangle covering both L_c and R_c can avoid the uncovered square by not extending to that row. However, a rectangle covering both L_c and R_c must be contiguous rows from min(row(L_c), row(R_c)) to max(row(L_c), row(R_c)). That interval includes all rows between them inclusive. If the uncovered square's row is not in that interval, then it's fine. So the condition for the rectangle to avoid the uncovered square is that the uncovered square's row is not between the rows of L_c and R_c. So the lower bound argument would be invalid if there exists a permutation where for some interior column c, the row of the uncovered square in column c is not between the rows of the uncovered squares in columns c-1 and c+1. In such a case, a single vertical tile could cover both L_c and R_c, reducing the number of tiles needed.\n\nThus the lower bound argument would only hold if for each interior column c, the row of the uncovered square in column c lies between the rows of the uncovered squares in columns c-1 and c+1. Is that always true? Not necessarily. Consider a permutation where σ is identity: σ(i)=i. Then rows are same as columns. For column c, we have uncovered square at (c, c). L_c is at (c+1, c). R_c is at (c-1, c). So rows are c+1 and c-1; the uncovered square row is c. Indeed, c is between c-1 and c+1. So for identity permutation, the condition holds. For other permutations, does it always hold? Let's test a simple example: n=3. Permutation σ: (1→2, 2→3, 3→1). So σ(1)=2, σ(2)=3, σ(3)=1. Then σ^{-1}(1)=3, σ^{-1}(2)=1, σ^{-1}(3)=2. So uncovered squares: (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). For column c=2, uncovered square at (σ^{-1}(2),2) = (1,2). L_2 = (σ^{-1}(3),2) = (2,2). R_2 = (σ^{-1}(1),2) = (3,2). So rows: L_2 row=2, uncovered row=1, R_2 row=3. So uncovered row 1 is not between rows 2 and 3; it's below both. So a vertical tile covering column 2 could cover both L_2 (row2) and R_2 (row3) without covering row1 (uncovered). Indeed, a vertical tile covering rows 2-3 in column 2 would avoid the uncovered square at row1. So a single tile could cover both L_2 and R_2. So the lower bound argument fails for this permutation. However, the lower bound argument might be applied to any permutation; they claim that for each interior column c, the two critical cells L_c and R_c must be covered by distinct tiles. But we found a counterexample where they could be covered by the same tile. So the lower bound is not valid for all permutations. However, the lower bound might still hold because the argument might be that each interior column c forces at least two distinct tiles overall, not necessarily covering L_c and R_c separately. Perhaps there is some other reason that each column forces at least two tiles, even if L_c and R_c could be covered by the same tile, maybe other constraints force at least two tiles anyway. Let's examine the argument more carefully.\n\nThey consider a tile T containing L_c. Since L_c is in column c, and the uncovered square in row σ^{-1}(c+1) is at column c+1, tile cannot include column c+1. So the rightmost column of T is c. Similarly, for tile containing R_c, leftmost column is c. So any tile covering L_c must have rightmost column c, any tile covering R_c must have leftmost column c. Could a tile cover both L_c and R_c? It would need to have leftmost column c and rightmost column c, i.e., width 1. So it's a vertical tile in column c. So the only way to cover both L_c and R_c with the same tile is to have a vertical tile in column c. So the question reduces to: can we have a vertical tile in column c that covers both L_c and R_c while avoiding uncovered squares? As we argued, yes if the uncovered square in column c is not between the rows of L_c and R_c. So the lower bound argument fails for permutations where that condition holds.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 is not universally valid; it's only valid for permutations where each column's uncovered square lies between the rows of the uncovered squares in adjacent columns. But the solution uses identity permutation for construction, which satisfies that condition. However, the lower bound must hold for any configuration of uncovered squares (any permutation). The problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve the condition that each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. The uncovered squares can be arranged arbitrarily (any permutation). The goal is to minimize the number of tiles needed, given we can choose the arrangement of uncovered squares as part of the solution? Wait, the problem statement: \"Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nInterpretation: Matilda can place tiles arbitrarily, but she also must ensure that each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square. She can choose which squares are uncovered (i.e., she can decide which squares to leave uncovered). The problem asks: what is the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve that condition? So she can choose the arrangement of uncovered squares to minimize the number of tiles. So the lower bound must hold for any arrangement of uncovered squares, but we can choose the arrangement that yields the minimal number of tiles. So the lower bound must be a lower bound on the number of tiles needed for any possible arrangement. If there exists an arrangement that can be tiled with fewer than 2n-2 tiles, then the lower bound is not tight. The solution claims that 2n-2 is minimal for any arrangement, but we found a potential arrangement (permutation 1→2,2→3,3→1 for n=3) that might be tiled with fewer than 2n-2 = 4 tiles? Let's test n=3. According to solution, minimal number of tiles would be 2*3-2 = 4. But maybe we can tile the complement of that permutation with 3 tiles or less. Let's try to tile n=3 with uncovered squares at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). The complement squares are all other squares: (1,1), (1,3), (2,1), (2,2), (3,2), (3,3). Let's see if we can tile them with 3 rectangles. For instance, we could have a vertical tile covering column 1 rows 2-3 (cells (2,1),(3,1)). But (3,1) is uncovered! Wait, uncovered squares: (3,1) is uncovered, so cannot cover that. So vertical tile in column 1 cannot include row 3. So maybe tile covering column 1 row 2 only (cell (2,1)). That's a 1x1 tile, but we can have many small tiles. But we want minimal number of tiles. Let's try to find a tiling with 3 rectangles. The uncovered squares are at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). The complement squares are:\n\nRow1: (1,1) and (1,3)\nRow2: (2,1) and (2,2)\nRow3: (3,2) and (3,3)\n\nWe could tile (1,1) and (2,1) together as a vertical tile covering column 1 rows 1-2 (since (3,1) is uncovered, we cannot include row3). That tile covers (1,1),(2,1). Good.\n\nWe could tile (1,3) and (2,2) and (3,2) and (3,3) maybe as a rectangle? Let's see: (1,3) is at row1 col3, (2,2) row2 col2, (3,2) row3 col2, (3,3) row3 col3. Not a rectangle. Could tile (1,3) alone, (2,2) alone, (3,2),(3,3) together as a horizontal tile covering row3 columns 2-3. That would be 4 tiles: vertical tile (col1 rows1-2), horizontal tile (row3 col2-3), singletons (1,3) and (2,2). That's 4 tiles. Could we do better? Maybe we can tile (1,3) and (2,2) as a diagonal? No, rectangles must be axis-aligned. Could tile (1,3) and (2,2) and (3,2) as a shape? Not rectangle. Could tile (2,2) and (3,2) as vertical tile column2 rows2-3 (but (3,2) is covered, okay). However, (3,2) is covered, but (3,2) is not uncovered. So vertical tile covering column2 rows2-3 includes (2,2) and (3,2). That's okay. Then we have (1,3) and (3,3) left. Could tile (1,3) and (3,3) together? Not rectangle because missing (2,3) which is uncovered. So must tile them separately: (1,3) as a 1x1 tile, (3,3) as a 1x1 tile. So total tiles: vertical tile col1 rows1-2, vertical tile col2 rows2-3, tile (1,3), tile (3,3). That's 4 tiles. So maybe 4 is minimal for this arrangement. Could we tile with 3? Let's try other arrangement: maybe a horizontal tile covering row1 columns1-3 except column2 (uncovered). But you can't have a hole in a rectangle. So not possible. So maybe 4 is minimal for n=3. So lower bound 2n-2 = 4 holds for n=3. But we need to test for larger n if there exists a permutation that can be tiled with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles. Let's try n=4. Lower bound would be 6. Could we tile with 5? Possibly. Let's try to find a permutation that might allow a vertical tile covering both L_c and R_c for some columns, reducing tile count. But we need to check overall tile count.\n\nWe need to examine the known result: The rectangle covering number of the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly 2n-2. I think this is a known combinatorial geometry result. Let's recall known results: \"A permutation matrix of size n can be partitioned into at most n-1 monotone subsequences (Erdos–Szekeres). But that's about monotone subsequences. Not relevant.\n\nThere is a known problem: \"Cover all cells of an n x n board except for a rook placement (one per row and column) with the minimum number of rectangles.\" I think the answer is 2n-2. The proof uses the concept of \"critical cells\" similar to the solution. The argument that each interior column forces at least two distinct rectangles is standard. However, we need to verify if the argument about width-1 tiles is correct. Let's examine the typical proof.\n\nI recall a known solution: For each column c (except first and last), there are two \"critical\" cells: the cell immediately left of the uncovered cell in column c+1, and the cell immediately right of the uncovered cell in column c-1. These are in column c. The claim is that any rectangle covering the left critical cell cannot extend to column c+1 (because that column contains the uncovered cell in that row). So its rightmost column is c. Similarly, any rectangle covering the right critical cell cannot extend to column c-1, so its leftmost column is c. Thus any rectangle covering both critical cells would have leftmost column c and rightmost column c, i.e., width 1. But a width-1 rectangle covering both critical cells would also cover the uncovered cell in column c, because the uncovered cell lies between them in the same column. However, is that always true? Let's think: The left critical cell is left of the uncovered cell in column c+1, which is in the same row as that uncovered cell. The right critical cell is right of the uncovered cell in column c-1, which is in the same row as that uncovered cell. The uncovered cell in column c is in column c, but its row may be different from the rows of the two critical cells. The claim that the uncovered cell lies between them in the same column is not obvious. Let's draw a diagram.\n\nWe have a permutation matrix: each row i has uncovered cell at column σ(i). So we have points (i, σ(i)). For column c, we have uncovered cell at (i_c, c) where i_c = σ^{-1}(c). For column c+1, uncovered cell at (i_{c+1}, c+1). The left critical cell L_c is at (i_{c+1}, c). So it's in column c, row i_{c+1}. For column c-1, uncovered cell at (i_{c-1}, c-1). The right critical cell R_c is at (i_{c-1}, c). So it's in column c, row i_{c-1}. So the two critical cells are at rows i_{c+1} and i_{c-1} in column c. The uncovered cell in column c is at row i_c. So we have three rows: i_{c-1}, i_c, i_{c+1}. The claim is that i_c lies between i_{c-1} and i_{c+1}. Is that always true? Not necessarily. However, maybe there is a property of permutations: For any three consecutive columns c-1, c, c+1, the rows of the uncovered squares cannot be monotonic? Actually, there is no such restriction. Permutations can have any ordering of rows across columns. So i_c could be anywhere relative to i_{c-1} and i_{c+1}. So the claim that i_c lies between i_{c-1} and i_{c+1} is false in general.\n\nThus the standard lower bound argument must be refined: perhaps they consider not just column c but also rows, and they show that each interior column forces at least two distinct tiles, but not necessarily covering L_c and R_c separately; maybe there is a different argument that ensures at least two tiles per column regardless of ordering. Let's think about alternative arguments.\n\nOne known argument: In any covering of the complement of a permutation matrix, each column must be \"split\" into at least two intervals of covered cells (except possibly first and last columns). Because the uncovered cell in column c splits the column into two parts: above and below the uncovered cell. The covered cells in that column are all cells except the uncovered one. So the column consists of two intervals: rows 1..i_c-1 (above) and rows i_c+1..n (below). Each of these intervals must be covered by tiles that have that column as either leftmost or rightmost column (or interior). But maybe each interval must be covered by at least one tile. However, a tile could cover both intervals if it extends horizontally to avoid the uncovered cell? Let's examine.\n\nConsider column c. The uncovered cell at (i_c, c) splits the column into two sets: cells above (rows < i_c) and cells below (rows > i_c). Any tile covering a cell above the uncovered cell cannot extend to column c+1 if it includes the row i_c+1? Wait, we need to think about the shape of tiles. Tiles are rectangles. If a tile includes a cell above the uncovered cell in column c, it could also include cells in columns to the right or left, but must avoid any uncovered cells. The uncovered cell in column c+1 is at row i_{c+1}. If the tile includes column c+1, it must avoid the uncovered cell at (i_{c+1}, c+1). So the tile's rows must avoid that row. So the tile covering a cell above the uncovered cell in column c could extend horizontally only if its row range does not include i_{c+1}. Similarly for column c-1 and i_{c-1}.\n\nThus perhaps each column c must have at least two tiles covering its above and below intervals, because any tile covering a cell above cannot also cover a cell below due to the uncovered cell in the middle. However, a tile could be tall enough to cover both intervals if it avoids the uncovered cell by not including that row, but that would require the tile to have a hole, which is not allowed. Since a rectangle cannot",
