claims lower bound 2n-2 and constructs a tiling achieving that bound, using identity permutation (diagonal uncovered). They use vertical tiles covering all squares below diagonal in each column (except diagonal) and horizontal tiles covering all squares to the right of diagonal in each row (except diagonal). That yields n-1 vertical tiles (columns 1 to n-1) each covering squares below diagonal in that column, and n-1 horizontal tiles (rows 1 to n-1) each covering squares to the right of diagonal in that row. So total 2n-2 tiles. Indeed, they cover all non-diagonal squares exactly once: For any cell (i,j) with i>j, it's covered by vertical tile V_j (since column j, rows > j). For any cell (i,j) with i<j, it's covered by horizontal tile H_i (since row i, columns > i). The diagonal squares are uncovered. So this is a valid tiling.\n\nNow, is 2n-2 minimal? The solution provides a lower bound argument using \"critical cells\" L_c and R_c. They argue each interior column c (2 ≤ c ≤ n-1) forces at least two distinct tiles: one with rightmost column c (covering L_c) and one with leftmost column c (covering R_c). They argue a tile cannot have both leftmost and rightmost column c unless width 1, and a width-1 tile can contain at most one of L_c, R_c. So each interior column forces at least two distinct tiles. Border columns force at least one tile each. So total at least 2n-2 tiles.\n\nWe need to verify the correctness of this lower bound argument. It seems plausible. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe have uncovered squares at positions (i, σ(i)). For each column c, they define L_c = (σ^{-1}(c+1), c) which is the cell immediately left of the uncovered square in column c+1 (i.e., the uncovered square in column c+1 is at row σ^{-1}(c+1), column c+1). So L_c is the cell in the same row but column c. Similarly, R_c = (σ^{-1}(c-1), c) is the cell immediately right of the uncovered square in column c-1 (the uncovered square in column c-1 is at row σ^{-1}(c-1), column c-1). So R_c is the cell in the same row but column c.\n\nThus L_c is the rightmost covered cell in the row of the uncovered square at column c+1 (since it's the cell left of that uncovered square). Similarly, R_c is the leftmost covered cell in the row of the uncovered square at column c-1 (since it's the cell right of that uncovered square). So each column c (except border) has two critical cells: L_c and R_c. For border columns c=1 and c=n, only one of them exists: L_1 (leftmost column has uncovered square at column 2's row, so L_1 is left of that uncovered square) and R_n (rightmost column has uncovered square at column n-1's row, so R_n is right of that uncovered square). So total critical cells count = 2n-2.\n\nNow they argue each tile containing L_c must have rightmost column exactly c (since column c+1 cannot be part of tile because that column contains the uncovered square in that row). Similarly, each tile containing R_c must have leftmost column exactly c (since column c-1 cannot be part of tile). So each interior column c requires at least one tile with rightmost column c (covering L_c) and at least one tile with leftmost column c (covering R_c). Could the same tile satisfy both? That would require a tile whose leftmost column = rightmost column = c, i.e., width 1. A width-1 tile is a vertical strip covering only column c. They argue such a tile can contain at most one of L_c and R_c because if it contained both, then it would contain the uncovered square (σ^{-1}(c), c) which is not allowed. Actually, L_c is at column c, row σ^{-1}(c+1). R_c is at column c, row σ^{-1}(c-1). The uncovered square in column c is at row σ^{-1}(c), column c. So a vertical tile covering column c would cover a set of rows. It could possibly include both L_c and R_c if it includes rows σ^{-1}(c+1) and σ^{-1}(c-1). But if it includes the row σ^{-1}(c) (the uncovered square), that would be illegal. However, a vertical tile could be chosen to avoid that row, e.g., covering rows above σ^{-1}(c)