1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and a set of columns \\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with the property\n\\[\nR\\cap C=\\varnothing .\n\\]\nThe tile covers exactly the squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\in R,\\;j\\in C\\}\\); in the graph language this is the complete bipartite subgraph \\(K_{R,C}\\).\n\nLet \\(\\mathbf 1_R\\in\\{0,1\\}^n\\) be the characteristic vector of \\(R\\) and \\(\\mathbf 1_C\\) that of \\(C\\).  \nThe incidence matrix of the tile is the outer product\n\\[\nM_{R,C}= \\mathbf 1_R\\,\\mathbf 1_C^{\\mathsf T},\n\\]\nwhich has rank at most 1 (rank 1 if both \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are non‑empty).\n\nIf the whole complement \\(E\\) is partitioned into \\(t\\) tiles \\((R_k,C_k)\\) \\((k=1,\\dots ,t)\\), then the adjacency matrix \\(A\\) of \\(H_n\\) satisfies\n\\[\nA=\\sum_{k=1}^{t} M_{R_k,C_k}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Rank of the adjacency matrix  \n\nWrite \\(J\\) for the \\(n\\times n\\) all‑ones matrix and \\(P\\) for the permutation matrix of \\(\\sigma\\) (i.e. \\(P_{i,\\sigma(i)}=1\\) and all other entries are 0).  \nBy the definition of the uncovered squares,\n\\[\nA = J - P .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n**Lemma 1.** For every permutation matrix \\(P\\) of size \\(n>1\\),\n\\[\n\\det(J-P)=(-1)^{\\,n-1}(n-1)\\neq0 .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* Subtract the first row from each of the remaining \\(n-1\\) rows; the determinant becomes\n\\[\n\\det\\!\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 1 & \\dots & 1\\\\\n0 & -p_{22} & \\dots & -p_{2n}\\\\\n\\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots\\\\\n0 & -p_{n2} & \\dots & -p_{nn}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n= (-1)^{\\,n-1}\\det(P_{[2..n],[2..n]}) .\n\\]\nSince \\(P\\) is a permutation matrix, the submatrix obtained by deleting the first row and first column is again a permutation matrix of size \\(n-1\\) and therefore has determinant \\((-1)^{\\,n-2}\\). Multiplying the signs yields \\((-1)^{\\,n-1}(n-1)\\). ∎\n\nConsequently \\(J-P\\) is invertible, so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)=\\operatorname{rank}(J-P)=n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEach matrix \\(M_{R_k,C_k}\\) in (1) has rank at most 1.  \nTherefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(A)\\le\\sum_{k=1}^{t}\\operatorname{rank}(M_{R_k,C_k})\\le t .\n\\]\nUsing (3) we obtain\n\\[\nt\\ge n .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus **any** tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions uses at least \\(n\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  An explicit tiling with \\(n\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) place a single rectangular tile\n\\[\nR_i=\\{i\\},\\qquad\nC_i=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThe tile is a \\(1\\times (n-1)\\) rectangle covering all squares of row \\(i\\) except the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nBecause the uncovered squares lie in distinct columns, the column sets \\(C_i\\) are pairwise disjoint, hence the \\(n\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square that is not a hole.\n\nThus a tiling with exactly \\(n\\) tiles exists, i.e.\n\\[\nt\\le n .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (4) and (5) we have \\(t=n\\).  \nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\), the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2025}.\n\\]"
