rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n... full proof.\n\nNow we need to ensure we use TeX for all math.\n\nNow we need to produce final answer.\n\nNow we need to check for any missing details: The lower bound argument uses the fact that each row r_k is unique for each k, which is true because σ is a permutation. The argument that we can extend tile to left border uses that for any row i in the tile's row interval, σ(i) > k, so column 1 is left of the hole. This holds because the tile's column interval is subset of [1,k] and includes column k, so the tile cannot include any row with σ(i) ≤ k, else column k would be to the right of the hole? Actually if σ(i) ≤ k, then the hole in row i is at column ≤ k, which would be inside the tile's column interval [1,k] (since the tile includes column k). That would be a hole inside the tile, contradiction. So indeed all rows in the tile's interval satisfy σ(i) > k. So extending leftwards is safe.\n\nThus the argument is solid.\n\nNow we need to produce final answer.\n\nNow we need to ensure we mention that n = 2025.\n\nNow we need to compute 2*2025 - 2 = 4050 - 2 = 4048.\n\nNow we need to present final answer as \\boxed{4048}.\n\nNow we need to produce final output.\n\nNow we need to double-check for any missing steps: The lower bound argument also requires that the right-anchored tiles are distinct and at least n-1. The symmetric argument uses rows s_k such that σ(s_k) = n - k (i.e., hole at column n - k). The squares to the right of the hole are columns n - k + 1 .. n. The rightmost square right of the hole is (s_k, n - k + 1). By analogous reasoning, we get a right-anchored tile covering columns n - k + 1 .. n. Distinctness follows similarly.\n\nThus lower bound holds.\n\nNow we need to ensure we mention that for n=1, the answer is 0 (but not needed for n=2025). But we can mention for completeness.\n\nNow we need to produce final answer.\n\nNow we need to ensure we have no internal commentary.\n\nNow we will write the final answer.\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation of uncovered squares.**  \n   Because each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n2. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   For each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(r_k\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(r_k)=k+1\\).  \n   The square \\((r_k,k)\\) is the rightmost square left of the uncovered one in that row.  \n   Any tile covering \\((r_k,k)\\) must have its column interval contained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and must contain column \\(k\\); otherwise it would also contain the uncovered square \\((r_k,k+1)\\).  \n   Consequently the tile’s column interval can be extended leftwards to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) without meeting a hole, producing a **left‑anchored** tile \\(L_k\\) covering columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   If two different values \\(k<\\ell\\) used the same left‑anchored tile, that tile would contain column \\(k+1\\) in row \\(r_k\\), contradicting the fact that \\((r_k,k+1)\\) is uncovered. Hence the tiles \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are all distinct; at least \\(n-1\\) left‑anchored tiles are necessary.\n\n   By symmetry, considering the squares immediately to the right of each uncovered square yields at least \\(n-1\\) **right‑anchored** tiles \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_{n-1}\\) (each covering columns \\(\\ell,\\dots ,n\\) for some \\(\\ell\\)).  \n   A tile cannot be both left‑ and right‑anchored, so any tiling uses at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n   tiles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (uncovered squares on the main diagonal).  \n   For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define  \n\n   * a vertical tile  \n     \\[\n     V_k=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\((n-k)\\times1\\) rectangle lying strictly left of the diagonal;\n\n   * a horizontal tile  \n     \\[\n     H_k=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n     \\]\n     a \\(1\\times(n-k)\\) rectangle lying strictly above the diagonal.\n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) tiles \\(\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint (they intersect only at the diagonal squares, which are uncovered) and together cover every non‑diagonal square. Hence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction with \\(2n-2\\) tiles show that the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For the given board \\(n=2025\\), the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows of the board by \\(R_1,\\dots ,R_n\\) and the columns by \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nSince each row contains exactly one uncovered unit square and each column also contains exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|U|=n\\) and the set of squares that must be tiled is  \n\n\\[\n\\overline U=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_k\\;:=\\;\\text{the unique row with }\\sigma(r_k)=k+1 .\n\\]\n\n(The existence and uniqueness follow from the fact that \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.)\n\nIn row \\(r_k\\) the uncovered square is \\((r_k,k+1)\\); the squares left of it are\n\\((r_k,1),\\dots ,(r_k,k)\\).  \nThe rightmost of those, \\((r_k,k)\\), must be covered by some tile; call this tile \\(T_k\\).\n\n*Because a tile may not contain an uncovered square, the column interval of \\(T_k\\) cannot contain column \\(k+1\\). Hence the column interval of \\(T_k\\) is a subset of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and must contain column \\(k\\). Consequently the rightmost column of \\(T_k\\) equals \\(k\\).*\n\nLet the row interval of \\(T_k\\) be \\(I_k\\).  \nIf a row \\(i\\in I_k\\) satisfied \\(\\sigma(i)\\le k\\), then column \\(k\\) would lie to the right of the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) and therefore the hole would belong to the tile—impossible. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in I_k\\qquad\\sigma(i)>k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1) we may enlarge the column interval of \\(T_k\\) leftwards to the whole set \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) without meeting a hole. Replacing \\(T_k\\) by this enlarged tile does not increase the number of tiles, so we may assume that for each \\(k\\) there exists a **left‑anchored** tile\n\n\\[\nL_k\\;:\\;\\text{columns }1,\\dots ,k\\text{ and rows }I_k\\;(I_k\\text{ a consecutive set of rows containing }r_k). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nNow suppose that for two different numbers \\(k<\\ell\\) the same left‑anchored tile were used. Then that tile would have column interval \\(\\{1,\\dots ,\\ell\\}\\) and would contain the square \\((r_k,k+1)\\), which is uncovered—contradiction. Hence the tiles \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\) are all distinct. Consequently any tiling uses at least \\(n-1\\) left‑anchored tiles.\n\nApplying the same reasoning to the squares immediately to the **right** of each uncovered square yields a family of **right‑anchored** tiles.  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(s_k\\) be the unique row with \\(\\sigma(s_k)=n-k\\).  \nThe square \\((s_k,n-k+1)\\) is the leftmost square right of the uncovered one in that row; an argument symmetric to the one above shows that a distinct right‑anchored tile covering columns \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\) is required. Thus at least \\(n-1\\) right‑anchored tiles are necessary.\n\nA tile cannot be both left‑anchored (its rightmost column \\(\\le n-1\\)) and right‑anchored (its leftmost column \\(\\ge 2\\)), because such a rectangle would contain the whole row and therefore an uncovered square. Hence the two families are disjoint.\n\nSummarising,\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ tiles}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_k&:=\\{(i,k)\\mid i=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{(a vertical }(n-k)\\times1\\text{ rectangle)},\\\\[2pt]\nH_k&:=\\{(k,j)\\mid j=k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{(a horizontal }1\\times(n-k)\\text{ rectangle)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Properties of the tiles.*\n\n*   Each \\(V_k\\) lies strictly to the left of the diagonal, because for any row \\(i>k\\) the uncovered square in that row is \\((i,i)\\) with column \\(i>k\\); thus column \\(k\\) is left of the hole and \\(V_k\\) contains no uncovered square.\n*   Each \\(H_k\\) lies strictly above the diagonal, because in row \\(k\\) the uncovered square is \\((k,k)\\) and all columns \\(j>k\\) are to the right of that hole.\n*   The sets \\(\\{V_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different columns), the sets \\(\\{H_k\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint (different rows), and a vertical tile \\(V_k\\) never meets a horizontal tile \\(H_\\ell\\) because their only possible intersection would be the square \\((k,k)\\), which is excluded from both families. Hence all \\(2n-2\\) tiles are disjoint.\n*   Every non‑diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one of the tiles: if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\); if \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).\n\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) tiles \\(\\{V_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\cup\\{H_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) cover precisely the set \\(\\overline U\\) of squares that must be tiled.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe lower bound (3) shows that any admissible placement of tiles uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.