xcept the prescribed uncovered ones, and satisfy the row/column condition.  \n\nSince the lower and upper bounds coincide, the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(f(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\na_i:=f(i)-1\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad b_i:=n-f(i)\\qquad(1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(a_i\\) is the number of covered squares to the left of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\), and \\(b_i\\) is the number of covered squares to the right.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\n\\{a_1,\\dots ,a_n\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}=\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_n\\},\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbecause the numbers \\(f(i)\\) run through \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) exactly once.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the left side\n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\]\n\nthe collection of all covered squares that lie **left** of the uncovered squares.  \nA rectangle that lies inside \\(L\\) must be of the form  \n\n\\[\nI\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,w\\},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a non‑empty interval of rows and \\(w\\ge1\\) is an integer.  \nIndeed, any rectangle in \\(L\\) must start at column 1 (otherwise it would contain a column that is absent from some row of the rectangle).\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there is exactly one index  \n\n\\[\nr_k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\quad\\text{with}\\quad a_{r_k}=k,\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nby (1).  \nThe cell \\((r_k,k)\\) is the **rightmost** cell of row \\(r_k\\) that belongs to \\(L\\).  \nIf a rectangle of the form (2) contains \\((r_k,k)\\), its width \\(w\\) must satisfy \\(w\\ge k\\).  \nIf \\(w>k\\), the