n n. Let's think: what is the actual minimal number of tiles? Let's analyze the problem ourselves to see if answer 2025 is indeed correct or not.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangular tiles covering all but exactly one square per row and per column. Each row and column has exactly one uncovered square. So there are n uncovered squares, one per row and column. So the uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: exactly one per row and column.\n\nWe need to cover all other squares with rectangles that do not include any uncovered squares. Tiles cannot overlap (each unit square covered at most once). So we need to partition the remaining squares (the complement of the permutation matrix) into rectangles (axis-aligned). The question: what is the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix? This is a known combinatorial problem: partition of a permutation matrix complement into rectangles (or \"grid graph\" with holes). The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (the size of the matrix). But is that always possible? Let's think.\n\nWe have an n x n board with a permutation of n holes (uncovered squares). The complement is the set of all other squares. We need to partition this set into axis-aligned rectangles (submatrices) that do not contain any hole. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n: you can partition each row into at most two intervals: left of the hole and right of the hole. But each interval is a rectangle (since it's a single row). However, you cannot combine intervals across rows because holes are in different columns. But you could combine rows that have holes in the same column? No, each column has exactly one hole, so each column also has a hole at some row. So each column also splits the column into two intervals: above and below the hole. So the complement can be partitioned into rectangles that are \"staircase\" shapes? Let's think.\n\nWe can think of the complement as the union of all cells (i,j) where j != pi(i). We need to partition into rectangles. A rectangle is defined by a set of consecutive rows R = {a,...,b} and consecutive columns C = {c,...,d}. The rectangle includes all cells (i,j) with i in R, j in C. The rectangle must not contain any uncovered square (i, pi(i)). So for any rectangle, for each row i in R, we must have pi(i) not in C. So C must avoid all pi(i) for i in R. Similarly, for each column j in C, we must have pi^{-1}(j) not in R. So the rectangle must avoid the set of points (i, pi(i)). So we need to partition the complement into rectangles that avoid the permutation points.\n\nThe minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be n (the size of the permutation). I recall a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into n rectangles (or monotone rectangles). Actually, there is a known decomposition: For each i from 1 to n, consider the rectangle formed by rows i..n and columns pi(i)+1..n (if we order rows and columns appropriately). But that might not be a partition. Let's think.\n\nAlternatively, we can think of the complement as a union of two monotone partitions: For each i, the rectangle covering all cells to the right of the hole in row i (i.e., columns pi(i)+1..n). That's a rectangle of size 1 x (n - pi(i)). Similarly, cells to the left of the hole: columns 1..pi(i)-1. That's also a rectangle of size 1 x (pi(i)-1). So each row can be covered by up to two rectangles. So total number of rectangles needed is at most 2n (if we use both left and right intervals). But we can possibly combine intervals across rows to reduce the number. For example, if the holes are arranged in a monotone increasing permutation (i.e., pi(i) = i), then the left intervals are columns 1..i-1, which are nested: row i's left interval is a superset of row i+1's left interval? Actually, pi(i) = i, then left interval for row i is columns 1..i-1. So row i+1's left interval is columns 1..i. So they are not nested but overlapping. We could combine rows i and i+1 into a rectangle covering columns 1..i-1 for rows i..i+1? But that rectangle would include the hole at (i+1,i+1) if column i+1 is not included. Actually, if we take rows i..i+1 and columns 1..i-1, that rectangle does not include any hole because holes are at (i,i) and (i+1,i+1). Both are not in columns 1..i-1. So it's okay. So we could combine left intervals across rows to form larger rectangles. Similarly, right intervals can be combined.\n\nThus the minimal number of rectangles might be something like the size of the longest decreasing subsequence or something. There's known results: The minimal number of monotone (axis-aligned) rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix equals the size of the permutation (n). Actually, there is a known theorem: The permutation matrix can be partitioned into n non-attacking rooks, but that's trivial. But covering the complement with rectangles is akin to partitioning the permutation graph into comparability graphs? I'm not sure.\n\nLet's try to find a construction with n rectangles that works for any permutation. The solution's construction uses n rectangles each covering a whole row except the hole, but those are not rectangles because the columns are not contiguous. However, we could use rectangles that are not necessarily single rows: we could use rectangles that are the whole board except a \"staircase\" shape? Let's think.\n\nConsider the permutation matrix points as obstacles. We need to partition the remaining cells into rectangles that avoid obstacles. This is reminiscent of the problem of covering a board with rectangles avoiding a set of points. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a set of points is at most the number of points plus 1? Actually, for a set of points in general position, you can partition the board into at most (#points + 1) rectangles by drawing lines through each point? But we need rectangles that are axis-aligned and cannot contain any point.\n\nOne naive approach: For each point (i, pi(i)), draw a vertical line just to the left of the point (i.e., between columns pi(i)-1 and pi(i)), and a horizontal line just above the point (i.e., between rows i-1 and i). This partitions the board into a grid of rectangles that avoid the points. However, each point creates new rectangles, and the total number of rectangles could be up to (n+1)^2? Not minimal.\n\nBut we can do better: Use a \"staircase\" decomposition: For each i, define rectangle covering rows i..n and columns pi(i)+1..n. Those rectangles are nested: each rectangle is a submatrix of the previous one if pi(i) is increasing? Not necessarily.\n\nLet's examine the solution's rank argument: They claim rank(M) = n, thus need at least n tiles. That is correct: rank(M) = n, each tile is rank-1, sum of rank-1 matrices yields rank ≤ number of tiles. So lower bound is n. So any solution must have at least n tiles. So answer must be at least 2025. The question is whether n tiles are sufficient. The solution gave a construction that is invalid because tiles are not rectangles. So we need to see if there exists a valid construction with n rectangles. If not, the minimal number may be larger than n. Let's explore.\n\nWe need to find a partition of the complement of a permutation matrix into n rectangles. Is that possible? Let's try to construct for n=3 with a permutation (1,2,3) (identity). The board is 3x3 with holes at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). The complement squares are all off-diagonal cells. Can we partition them into 3 rectangles? Let's try: rectangle 1: rows 1, columns 2-3 (i.e., row 1, columns 2-3) is a rectangle (size 1x2). That's okay. Rectangle 2: rows 2, columns 1 and 3? That's not contiguous (columns 1 and 3). So we cannot cover row 2 with a single rectangle that excludes column 2. We could split row 2 into two rectangles: (row2, col1) and (row2, col3). That's two rectangles. So total rectangles would be at least 4. But maybe we can combine some cells across rows: For example, rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 3 (i.e., a vertical rectangle of size 2x1) includes cells (1,3) and (2,3). That's okay because neither of those are holes. Similarly, rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 1 (size 2x1) includes (2,1) and (3,1). That's okay. And rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 2 (size 3x1) includes (1,2), (2,2) (hole), (3,2). That includes a hole at (2,2), so not allowed. So we need to avoid holes.\n\nLet's try to partition the off-diagonal cells for n=3 with identity permutation using 3 rectangles. Is that possible? Let's attempt to find a decomposition. Off-diagonal cells are:\n\nRow1: (1,2), (1,3)\nRow2: (2,1), (2,3)\nRow3: (3,1), (3,2)\n\nWe need to partition these 6 cells into 3 rectangles. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned contiguous block. Let's try to find 3 rectangles covering them.\n\nOption: rectangle A: rows 1-3, columns 1 (vertical column 1). But column 1 includes (1,1) hole, (2,1), (3,1). That includes hole (1,1) which is not allowed. So can't use full column 1. Could use rows 2-3, column 1: rectangle B: rows 2-3, column 1 (cells (2,1), (3,1)). That's okay. Similarly, rectangle C: rows 1-2, column 3 (cells (1,3), (2,3)). That's okay. Then we have left cells (1,2) and (3,2). Those are in column 2. Could we combine them into a rectangle? They are rows 1 and 3, not contiguous. So cannot be a single rectangle. Could combine them with row2? Row2 column2 is a hole, so can't. So we need at least two more rectangles for (1,2) and (3,2) individually. So total rectangles = 4. So for n=3 identity permutation, we need at least 4 rectangles? Let's see if we can do better with a different permutation. For n=3, maybe we can achieve 3 rectangles with a different permutation. Let's test permutation (1,2,3) is identity. Permutation (1,3,2) i.e., holes at (1,1), (2,3), (3,2). Off-diagonal cells:\n\nRow1: (1,2), (1,3)\nRow2: (2,1), (2,2)\nRow3: (3,1), (3,3)\n\nNow can we partition into 3 rectangles? Let's try: rectangle A: rows 1-3, column 1 (cells (1,1) hole, (2,1), (3,1)). Contains hole (1,1) so not allowed. Could use rows 2-3, column 1: (2,1), (3,1). That's okay. Rectangle B: rows 1-2, column 2: (1,2), (2,2). Both are not holes? (2,2) is not a hole (hole at (2,3)). So okay. Rectangle C: rows 1-3, column 3: (1,3), (2,3) hole, (3,3). Contains hole (2,3) so not allowed. Could use rows 1,3 column 3: not contiguous. So need more rectangles. So maybe 4 again.\n\nLet's test n=2 identity permutation: holes at (1,1), (2,2). Off-diagonal cells: (1,2), (2,1). Can we partition into 2 rectangles? Yes: rectangle covering (1,2) alone (1x1) and rectangle covering (2,1) alone (1x1). That's 2 rectangles = n. So for n=2, answer n works.\n\nFor n=3, seems we need at least 4 rectangles > n. So the lower bound n is not tight for n=3. But maybe there is a construction with n rectangles for any n? Let's try n=3 with a different permutation: (2,1,3) holes at (1,2), (2,1), (3,3). Off-diagonal cells:\n\nRow1: (1,1) hole? No, (1,1) is not a hole (hole at (1,2)). So (1,1), (1,3)\nRow2: (2,2), (2,3)\nRow3: (3,1), (3,2)\n\nNow can we partition into 3 rectangles? Let's try: rectangle A: rows 1-2, columns 1 (cells (1,1), (2,1) hole). Contains hole (2,1). Not allowed. Could use rows 1, column 1: (1,1) alone. That's a rectangle. Then rectangle B: rows 2-3, column 3: (2,3), (3,3) hole? (3,3) is a hole. So not allowed. Could use rows 2, column 3: (2,3) alone. Then rectangle C: rows 3, columns 1-2: (3,1), (3,2) both not holes. That's a rectangle of size 1x2. That covers (3,1),(3,2). So far we used rectangles: (1,1) alone, (2,3) alone, (3,1-2) together. That's 3 rectangles covering (1,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Missing (1,3) and (2,2). Could we combine (1,3) and (2,2) into a rectangle? They are not aligned. Could combine (1,3) with (2,3) but (2,3) already covered. So need more rectangles. So again >3.\n\nThus for n=3, seems minimal number >3. Let's try to find known results. This is akin to the problem of partitioning the complement of a permutation matrix into a minimal number of \"monotone\" rectangles (i.e., axis-aligned). The minimal number is known to be the size of the permutation's \"grid number\"? Actually, there is a known concept: \"grid number\" or \"grid class\" of a permutation, where you partition the permutation diagram into a grid of monotone blocks. The complement might be partitioned into rectangles that avoid the points. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is equal to the size of the permutation's \"increasing/decreasing\" structure? Not sure.\n\nBut we can think of the problem as a covering of the complement by rectangles that avoid the points. Equivalent to a \"rectangular partition\" of the board with holes. This is similar to the problem of \"rectangular partition of a board with holes\" where each hole is a unit cell that must be left uncovered. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the board into rectangles that avoid the holes is known to be at most n, but maybe exactly n for any permutation? Let's search memory: There is a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into n rectangles such that each rectangle contains exactly one cell from each row or each column? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the solution's construction can be fixed: Instead of using a single rectangle per row, we can use a rectangle that covers all rows except one column, but we need to avoid holes in each column. Actually, we can use n rectangles each of size (n-1) x (n-1) that avoid one row and one column? Let's think: For each i, define rectangle covering all rows except row i and all columns except column i. That rectangle would be the set of cells (r,c) where r != i and c != i. Does that rectangle contain any hole? The holes are at (i, pi(i)). If we choose pi = identity, holes at (i,i). Then rectangle covering rows != i and columns != i does not contain (i,i) because both row and column i are excluded. However, does it contain any other hole? For j != i, hole at (j,j) is in rows != i (since j != i) and columns != i (since j != i). So rectangle includes holes at (j,j) for all j != i. That's not allowed. So that fails.\n\nWhat about rectangles that are \"staircase\" shapes: For each i, define rectangle covering rows i..n and columns pi(i)..n (or something). That rectangle includes the hole at (i, pi(i)) because column pi(i) is included. So not allowed.\n\nThus we need a more clever construction.\n\nLet's think about the lower bound: rank(M) = n, so at least n rectangles needed. For n=2, we can achieve n. For n=3, we suspect maybe need > n. But we haven't proven minimal > n. Let's try to find a construction with 3 rectangles for n=3. Maybe we missed a possibility.\n\nGoal: partition 6 off-diagonal cells into 3 rectangles. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned contiguous block of cells. Let's enumerate all possible rectangles in a 3x3 board that avoid holes (i.e., cannot contain any of the 3 holes). For identity permutation holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Let's list all possible rectangles that avoid these holes.\n\nRectangles are defined by row interval [r1,r2] and column interval [c1,c2]. The rectangle includes all cells (r,c) with r1 <= r <= r2, c1 <= c <= c2. It must not contain any hole.\n\nGiven holes at (i,i). So rectangle cannot contain any cell where r=c. So rectangle must avoid diagonal cells. So any rectangle that includes a diagonal cell is invalid. So we need rectangles that lie entirely above or below the diagonal (strictly). That is, either all cells have column > row (above diagonal) or column < row (below diagonal). However, a rectangle that straddles the diagonal (i.e., includes both above and below cells) would contain a diagonal cell because the rectangle includes all cells in the rectangular region, which includes the diagonal cell where row = column within the interval. For a rectangle with row interval [r1,r2] and column interval [c1,c2], if the intervals intersect (i.e., there exists i such that r1 <= i <= r2 and c1 <= i <= c2), then the rectangle includes (i,i). So to avoid diagonal, we need either c2 < r1 (all columns left of rows) or c1 > r2 (all columns right of rows). So rectangles must be entirely in the region above diagonal (c1 > r2) or below diagonal (c2 < r1). So they are \"strictly upper triangular\" or \"strictly lower triangular\" rectangles.\n\nThus the complement of diagonal can be partitioned into rectangles that are either wholly above or wholly below diagonal. The region above diagonal is a set of cells (i,j) with i<j. This region is a triangular shape. Similarly below diagonal is i>j.\n\nNow, can we partition the upper triangular region (including cells above diagonal) into rectangles? The upper triangular region can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: each rectangle can be defined by rows i..i (single row) and columns i+1..n (the rest of that row to the right). That's n-1 rectangles (each row i has a contiguous block from column i+1 to n). Similarly, the lower triangular region can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: each row i has columns 1..i-1 (left side). That's another n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 rectangles. But we can combine some of these rectangles across rows to reduce count. For example, the region above diagonal can be partitioned into rectangles that are \"staircase\" shapes: e.g., rectangle covering rows 1..k and columns k+1..n (i.e., top-left block above diagonal). That rectangle includes cells (i,j) where i <= k and j >= k+1. Does it contain any diagonal cell? For i <= k, j >= k+1, we have i <= k < k+1 <= j, so i < j, so no diagonal cells. So it's valid. Similarly, we can partition the above-diagonal region into rectangles of the form rows [1..i] and columns [i+1..n] for i = 1..n-1. That's n-1 rectangles, each covering a \"staircase\" region. Actually, those rectangles overlap? Let's check: For i=1, rows 1..1, columns 2..n => row1 columns 2..n. For i=2, rows 1..2, columns 3..n => includes row1 columns 3..n (which overlap with rectangle for i=1). So they are not disjoint. So we cannot use overlapping rectangles; we need a partition (disjoint). So we need to partition the region into disjoint rectangles. The natural partition is each row's right side as a rectangle (size 1 x (n-i)). That's n-1 rectangles. Similarly left side yields n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 rectangles.\n\nBut perhaps we can combine some left and right intervals across rows to reduce total number of rectangles to n. For n=3, we have upper region cells: (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). Lower region cells: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2). That's 3 cells each region. Can we partition each region into 1 rectangle? Upper region: cells (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). Is there a rectangle covering exactly those three cells? The rectangle rows 1..2, columns 2..3 includes cells (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3). It includes (2,2) which is a hole. So not allowed. Could we partition upper region into 2 rectangles and lower region into 1 rectangle, total 3? Let's try: Upper region: rectangle A: row1 columns 2..3 (size 1x2) covering (1,2),(1,3). Lower region: rectangle B: rows 2..3 columns 1 (size 2x1) covering (2,1),(3,1). Then we have leftover cell (3,2) (lower region) and (2,3) (upper region). They are not contiguous, so need separate rectangles. So total 4.\n\nThus for identity permutation n=3, minimal number of rectangles appears to be 4. Let's see if we can do better with a different permutation. For n=3, maybe some permutations allow partition into 3 rectangles. Let's test permutation (1,2,3) we saw needed 4. Permutation (1,3,2) we also seemed to need at least 4. Permutation (2,1,3) also seemed to need at least 4. Permutation (2,3,1) holes at (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Let's examine that. Off-diagonal cells are all cells except those three. The complement includes cells:\n\nRow1: (1,1),(1,3)\nRow2: (2,1),(2,2)\nRow3: (3,2),(3,3)\n\nWe need to partition these 6 cells into 3 rectangles. Let's try to find a partition. Maybe we can use rectangle covering rows 1-2, columns 1 (cells (1,1),(2,1)). That's okay (no holes). Rectangle covering rows 2-3, columns 2 (cells (2,2),(3,2)). That's okay (hole at (2,3) not included). Rectangle covering rows 1-3, columns 3 (cells (1,3),(2,3) hole, (3,3)). Contains hole (2,3). Not allowed. Could use rectangle covering rows 1, column 3: (1,3) alone. Then rectangle covering rows 3, column 3: (3,3) alone. Then we have rectangles: (1,1),(2,1) as one; (2,2),(3,2) as second; (1,3) as third; (3,3) as fourth. That's 4.\n\nMaybe we can combine (1,3) and (3,3) into a rectangle rows 1-3, column 3, but that includes (2,3) hole. So not allowed.\n\nThus seems for n=3, minimal number of rectangles is 4 > n. So the lower bound n is not tight for n=3. So answer 2025 may be wrong if the minimal number of tiles is >2025. However, maybe for larger n, we can achieve n rectangles. The identity permutation case for n=3 required >n. But maybe for n odd >2, minimal number is n+1? Let's explore known results.\n\nWe need to find minimal number of rectangles to partition the complement of a permutation matrix of size n. This is known as the \"rectangular covering number\" or \"grid number\" of the complement of a permutation diagram. There is a known theorem: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into at most n rectangles, and this bound is tight for some permutations. Actually, I recall a known result: The permutation matrix can be partitioned into n monotone (increasing) sequences, but not sure about rectangles.\n\nLet's search memory: The problem of covering the complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles is related to the concept of \"grid class of permutations\". A permutation can be represented by a diagram of points, and a \"grid\" representation partitions the diagram into a grid of monotone cells. However, we want to partition the complement, not the points. The complement is the set of cells not containing points. Partitioning the complement into rectangles that avoid the points is essentially a \"rectangular decomposition\" of the board with holes.\n\nI think there is a known result: The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly n. This can be achieved by a construction known as \"staircase decomposition\" using \"horizontal strips\" and \"vertical strips\" that avoid the points. But we need to verify.\n\nLet's attempt to construct n rectangles for any permutation. Idea: For each i from 1 to n, define rectangle R_i = rows i..n, columns pi(i)+1..n (if pi(i) < n). But that rectangle includes holes at rows > i? Let's check. For any row j > i, the rectangle includes column pi(i)+1..n. If pi(j) is in that column range, then the rectangle includes hole (j, pi(j)). So not allowed. So that fails.\n\nAlternative: Use \"Ferrers diagram\" approach: Represent the permutation as a set of points; the complement can be partitioned into \"Young diagram\" shape of cells above and below the permutation. The shape above the permutation (cells (i,j) with j > pi(i)) can be partitioned into rectangles by considering the \"upper envelope\" of the permutation. Similarly below. The number of rectangles needed to partition the above region is equal to the number of \"records\" (or \"right-to-left maxima\") of the permutation. Similarly for below region. The sum of these numbers is at most n (since each element can be a record in at most one direction). Actually, each element can be a left-to-right maximum or not. Let's think.\n\nDefine the \"upper region\" U = {(i,j) : j > pi(i)}. This region can be partitioned into rectangles by scanning rows from top to bottom: For each row i, the set of columns j > pi(i) is a contiguous interval from pi(i)+1 to n. However, these intervals may overlap across rows. We can partition U into rectangles by grouping consecutive rows where the intervals are nested. For example, if pi(i) is non-decreasing (i.e., permutation is increasing), then intervals are decreasing (since pi(i) = i, intervals are from i+1 to n). Those intervals are nested: interval for row i is superset of interval for row i+1. So we can partition U into rectangles by taking each \"layer\" of columns: The region U can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: For each column j from 2 to n, the set of rows i with pi(i) < j is a contiguous block (since pi(i) = i). Actually, for identity, the region U is the set of cells above diagonal. That region can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: each rectangle is column j, rows 1..j-1. That's vertical rectangles: column j, rows 1..j-1. That's n-1 rectangles. Similarly, the lower region L = {(i,j) : j < pi(i)} can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: each column j, rows j+1..n. That's also n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 rectangles. But we can combine some of these rectangles across columns to reduce total number? For identity, we cannot combine because any rectangle that includes both column j and column k (j < k) would include a diagonal cell (i = j = column j?), maybe not. Let's test: Suppose we take rectangle rows 1..j-1, columns j..k (where j<k). This rectangle includes cell (j,j) (since row j is not included because rows are up to j-1). Actually, rows are 1..j-1, columns j..k. For any column c in [j,k], row c is not included because rows only go up to j-1 (< c). So diagonal cells (i,i) are not included because i must be in rows and columns simultaneously. Since rows are up to j-1, diagonal cells with i >= j are not included. So rectangle rows 1..j-1, columns j..k does not contain any diagonal cell because any cell (i,i) would require i >= j (since column i >= j), but rows only go up to j-1. So it's safe. So we can combine columns j..k into a single rectangle covering rows 1..j-1. That recta