ber of tiles.**  \n   Adding (1) and (2) and using \\(a_0=0\\) yields the exact number of tiles required for the permutation \\(\\sigma\\):\n   \\[\n   T(\\sigma)=L(\\sigma)+R(\\sigma)\n            =(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|a_i-a_{i-1}\\bigr|\n            =(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Lower bound.**  \n   For any two distinct integers \\(x\\neq y\\) we have \\(|x-y|\\ge1\\).  \n   Therefore each term in the sum of (3) is at least \\(1\\), giving  \n   \\[\n   T(\\sigma)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Achieving the bound.**  \n   Equality in (4) holds exactly when \\(|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)|=1\\) for all \\(i\\ge2\\); i.e. when \\(\\sigma\\) is monotone (the identity or the reverse identity).  \n   For the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) the board can be tiled as follows:\n\n   - **Left side:** for each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):c+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le c\\},\n     \\]\n     which covers all cells left of the diagonal in columns \\(1,\\dots ,c\\).  \n   - **Right side:** for each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n     \\[\n     \\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n-c,\\;c+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n     which covers all cells right of the diagonal in columns \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n   The two families consist of \\(n-1\\) rectangles each, are pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes. Hence the identity permutation uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, showing the lower bound is tight.\n\n8. **Conclusion for the problem.**  \n   With \\(n=2025\\) we obtain the minimal possible number of tiles\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\nThus the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with a general \\(n\\times n\\) board; the final numeric substitution is made at the end.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nEach row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nConsequently there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nAll other cells must be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Left and right parts of the board  \n\nFor a fixed \\(\\sigma\\) define  \n\n\\[\na_i:=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le a_i\\le n-1),\\qquad \nb_i:=n-\\sigma(i)=(n-1)-a_i .\n\\]\n\nThe **left part** \\(L(\\sigma)\\) consists of the cells\n\\[\nL(\\sigma)=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\},\n\\]\ni.e. the squares strictly to the left of the hole in each row.  \nThe **right part** \\(R(\\sigma)\\) consists of the cells\n\\[\nR(\\sigma)=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;n-b_i+1\\le j\\le n\\}\n        =\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;a_i+2\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile is a rectangle whose interior contains no hole; therefore a tile is either completely contained in \\(L(\\sigma)\\) or completely contained in \\(R(\\sigma)\\).  \nThus the whole board is tiled by a family of left‑anchored rectangles (all of whose columns are of the form \\(\\{1,\\dots ,c\\}\\)) together with a family of right‑anchored rectangles (all of whose columns are of the form \\(\\{c+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Minimal number of left‑anchored rectangles  \n\nConsider only \\(L(\\sigma)\\).  \nFor a fixed column index \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_c:=\\{\\,i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}:a_i\\ge c\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_c\\) is the set of rows that contain column \\(c\\) in the left part.  \nBecause a rectangle must have a contiguous set of rows, column \\(c\\) can be covered only by rectangles whose row set is a **maximal interval** of \\(S_c\\).  \nHence the number of left‑anchored rectangles needed is exactly  \n\n\\[\nL(\\sigma)=\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\\bigl(\\text{number of maximal intervals of }S_c\\bigr).\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nWrite the binary sequence \\(f_i(c)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{a_i\\ge c\\}}\\).  \nA maximal interval of \\(S_c\\) begins at a row \\(i\\) precisely when  \n\n\\[\nf_i(c)=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad(i=1\\text{ or }f_{i-1}(c)=0).\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\text{number of intervals of }S_c\n   =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\mathbf 1_{\\{a_i\\ge c,\\;i=1\\text{ or }a_{i-1}<c\\}} .\n\\]\n\nSumming over all \\(c\\) and interchanging the order of summation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(\\sigma)\n   &=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\sum_{c=1}^{n-1}\n        \\mathbf 1_{\\{a_i\\ge c,\\;i=1\\text{ or }a_{i-1}<c\\}}  \\\\\n   &=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\n        \\bigl(\\underbrace{a_i}_{c\\le a_i}\n        -\\underbrace{\\max\\{a_{i-1},0\\}}_{c\\le a_{i-1}}\\bigr)^{+}   \\\\\n   &=a_1+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\{0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(x^{+}=\\max\\{0,x\\}\\).  \nFormula (3.2) is the **minimum** possible number of left‑anchored rectangles: the greedy construction that, whenever \\(a_i>a_{i-1}\\), creates a new rectangle of width \\(a_i-a_{i-1}\\) covering rows \\(i,i+1,\\dots\\) until a later drop occurs, uses exactly the number of rectangles counted in (3.2), and any partition must contain at least that many because each increase of the left‑height introduces a new column that cannot be covered by earlier rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.   Minimal number of right‑anchored rectangles  \n\nThe right part is the left part of the reversed permutation \\(\\sigma'(i)=n+1-\\sigma(i)\\).  \nWriting \\(b_i=n-\\sigma(i)=(n-1)-a_i\\) and applying (3.2) to the sequence \\((b_i)\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nR(\\sigma)=(n-1-a_n)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\max\\{0,a_{i-1}-a_i\\}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.   Total number of tiles for a given permutation  \n\nAdding (3.2) and (4.1) and using the convention \\(a_0=0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nT(\\sigma)&=L(\\sigma)+R(\\sigma)\\\\\n         &=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl(\\max\\{0,a_i-a_{i-1}\\}\n                                   +\\max\\{0,a_{i-1}-a_i\\}\\bigr)\\\\\n         &=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}|a_i-a_{i-1}|.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\), the differences satisfy \\(|a_i-a_{i-1}|=|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)|\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,T(\\sigma)=(n-1)+\\sum_{i=2}^{n}\\bigl|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)\\bigr|\\,}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5.2) is exact: the greedy constructions described in Sections 3 and 4 produce a tiling with exactly \\(T(\\sigma)\\) rectangles, and the lower‑bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.   Minimising over all permutations  \n\nFor any two distinct integers \\(x\\neq y\\) we have \\(|x-y|\\ge1\\).  \nConsequently each term in the sum of (5.2) is at least \\(1\\), giving the universal lower bound  \n\n\\[\nT(\\sigma)\\ge (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nEquality in (6.1) occurs iff \\(|\\sigma(i)-\\sigma(i-1)|=1\\) for every \\(i\\ge2\\); i.e. the permutation is monotone (either \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\) or \\(\\sigma(i)=n+1-i\\) for all \\(i\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.   A tiling attaining the bound  \n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the left‑anchored rectangle  \n\n\\[\nL_c=\\{(i,j):c+1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le c\\},\n\\]\n\nand the right‑anchored rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_c=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n-c,\\;c+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) consist of \\(n-1\\) rectangles each, are pairwise disjoint, and together cover every unit square except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes.  \nThus the id