erval, because the tile’s rows are consecutive.  \nTherefore the cells of \\(U_j\\) cannot be covered by a single tile together with any cell of \\(D_j\\); they require at least one tile each.  \nIf \\(r=1\\) then \\(U_j\\) is empty, so only one tile is needed for column \\(j\\); if \\(r=n\\) then \\(D_j\\) is empty, again only one tile is needed.  \n\nExactly two columns have a hole at the top or at the bottom: the column \\(\\sigma(1)\\) contains the hole in row \\(1\\) (so \\(r=1\\)), and the column \\(\\sigma(n)\\) contains the hole in row \\(n\\) (so \\(r=n\\)).  \nAll remaining \\(n-2\\) columns have a hole in an interior row, hence each of them needs **at least two** tiles (one for the upper interval, one for the lower interval).  \n\nConsequently any tiling of the board must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-2)+1+1 = 2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.  This is a universal lower bound, independent of the permutation \\(\\sigma\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the two contiguous blocks of squares that lie to the left and to the right of the uncovered square:\n\n\\[\nL_i:=\\{(i,j):1\\le j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i:=\\{(i,j):\\sigma(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) (i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\)) place a tile that covers exactly the cells of \\(L_i\\).  \nSince \\(L_i\\) consists of a single row and a consecutive set of columns, this tile is a \\(1\\times(\\sigma(i)-1)\\) rectangle, whose sides lie on grid lines.\n\nIf \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\) (i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\)) place a tile that covers exactly the cells of \\(R_i\\).  \nThis tile is a \\(1\\times(n-\\sigma(i))\\) rectangle.\n\nThe tiles belonging to different rows are disjoint because they occupy distinct rows; within a given row the left and right tiles are separated by the uncovered square, so they do not overlap.  Hence the collection of all such tiles is a valid tiling: