d, not covered. (3,3) is covered? Actually (3,3) is covered (since uncovered at (3,2)). Did we cover (3,3)? No, we haven't covered (3,3). (3,3) is a covered cell. Could we cover (3,3) with any tile? It is in row3 column3. Row3 has uncovered at column2, so column3 is to the right of uncovered cell. Row3 also has uncovered at column2, so row3's right block includes columns3-4. But column4 is covered by tile B (rows2-4, column4). So we cannot overlap. Could we adjust tile B to not include (3,4) maybe? Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, we could tile row3's right block (columns3-4) as a rectangle rows3, columns3-4 (size 1x2). But column4 row3 is already covered by tile B. So we need to reassign.\n\nLet's try a different tiling approach:\n\nGoal: minimize tiles. Let's try to tile the region above the permutation (cells above each uncovered cell) and region below separately.\n\nThe region above the permutation (cells where row < sigma^{-1}(col)) for reverse permutation is the set of cells above each uncovered cell. Since uncovered cells are on the anti-diagonal, the region above is the set of cells above the anti-diagonal (i.e., cells where i + j < n+1). Similarly, region below is cells where i + j > n+1. This shape is symmetric and can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles each of shape \"staircase\"? Actually, the region above the anti-diagonal can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: For k=1 to n-1, rectangle covering rows 1..k, columns (n+1 - k) .. n? Let's think: The anti-diagonal line runs from (1,n) to (n,1). The region above it (northwest) is a shape that is also a staircase shape. It can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: each rectangle is a \"corner\" shape. For n=4, region above includes cells: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3); (2,1),(2,2); (3,1). That's a triangular shape (like identity case but rotated). It can be partitioned into 3 rectangles: row1 columns1-3 (1x3), row2 columns1-2 (1x2), row3 column1 (1x1). That's 3 rectangles. Similarly region below can be partitioned into 3 rect