les.\n\nNow, is it minimal? The lower bound argument: For each column j, the uncovered square is at row r = sigma^{-1}(j). The column is split into upper and lower intervals. Since each tile covering any cell in column j must have contiguous rows, you cannot cover both intervals with a single tile. So you need at least one tile for each interval that is non-empty. So each column needs at least one tile for each non-empty interval. For columns where the uncovered square is at top (r=1) or bottom (r=n), there is only one interval, so at least one tile. For all other columns (n-2 columns), there are two non-empty intervals, so at least two tiles. So total tiles >= 2*(n-2) + 2 = 2n-2.\n\nThis seems correct. However, we need to check if there is any possibility to use a tile that covers cells from multiple columns simultaneously, reducing tile count. The lower bound argument considered each column individually, but a tile can span multiple columns, covering cells in multiple columns. The argument says: \"A rectangular tile covering any cell of column j must occupy a set of rows that is an interval, because the tile’s rows are consecutive.\" But a tile also occupies a set of columns that is an interval. So a tile covering some cells in column j can also cover cells in other columns. The argument that \"cells of U_j cannot be covered by a single tile together with any cell of D_j\" is correct because the tile's rows would have to be an interval containing both rows above and below the uncovered cell, which would also include the uncovered cell, which is not allowed. So any tile covering cells in column j cannot cross the uncovered cell. So any tile covering cells in column j must be either entirely in U_j or entirely in D_j. However, a tile could cover cells in U_j for multiple columns, or D_j for multiple columns. So the lower bound per column may overcount: a tile could cover the upper intervals of multiple columns simultaneously, thus reducing total tile count. The argu