t, and those cells cannot be covered together with the other column without hitting a missing square. Hence each non‑empty top strip forces its own tile. There are \\(n-1\\) such strips, giving a lower bound of \\(n-1\\) tiles.  \n   - An identical argument applied to the “bottom’’ strips \\(S_j^{\\text{bottom}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j<i\\le n\\}\\) (whose heights \\(b_j=n-r_j\\) are also pairwise distinct) yields another \\(n-1\\) necessary tiles.  \n\n   The top and bottom strips are disjoint, so the total number of tiles in any tiling is at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient, and the lower‑bound argument shows that fewer tiles are impossible. Therefore the minimum number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n\n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) gives \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\\qquad \nG=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) grid.  \nA **tile** is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; it consists of all squares \\((i,j)\\) with  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b,\\qquad c\\le j\\le d\n\\]\n\nfor some integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection; i.e. \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation.  \n\nHence the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\subset G .\n\\]\n\nAll other squares,  \n\n\\[\nC=G\\setminus U,\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by tiles, and the tiles must be pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor a column \\(j\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_j=\\pi^{-1}(j)\\qquad (1\\le j\\le n)\n\\]\n\nbe the row of the uncovered square in that column.  \nConsider the two (possibly empty) vertical strips\n\n\\[\nT_j^{\\text{top}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\},\\qquad\nT_j^{\\text{bottom}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nBoth sets are rectangles (they are \\(1\\times(r_j-1)\\) and \\(1\\times(n-r_j)\\) respectively) and contain no uncovered square, because the only square of column \\(j\\) that is uncovered is \\((r_j,j)\\).\n\nExactly one column has \\(r_j=1\\) (the column whose uncovered square lies in the top row); for that column \\(T_j^{\\text{top}}=\\varnothing\\).  \nExactly one column has \\(r_j=n\\); for that column \\(T_j^{\\text{bottom}}=\\varnothing\\).\n\nAll other strips are non‑empty, and they are pairwise disjoint because they lie in distinct columns.  \nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\bigl\\{T_j^{\\text{top}}\\mid r_j>1\\bigr\\}\\cup\n           \\bigl\\{T_j^{\\text{bottom}}\\mid r_j<n\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles and covers every square of \\(C\\).  \nThus\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{there exists a tiling with }2n-2\\text{ tiles}.}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Every tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe prove two separate lower bounds, one for the “top’’ strips and one for the “bottom’’ strips.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  Top strips  \n\nFor each column \\(j\\) define the **top strip**\n\n\\[\nS_j^{\\text{top}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i< r_j\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r_j=1\\) the strip is empty; otherwise it is a non‑empty rectangle.  \nBecause \\((r_j,j)\\) is uncovered, any rectangle that contains a cell of \\(S_j^{\\text{top}}\\) cannot extend to row \\(r_j\\); hence the whole strip \\(S_j^{\\text{top}}\\) is a *maximal* rectangle of the uncovered board.\n\nThe numbers \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently the heights  \n\n\\[\nh_j:=|S_j^{\\text{top}}|=r_j-1\\qquad (j=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare all distinct.  \nExactly one column has \\(h_j=0\\) (the column whose uncovered square lies in the top row), so there are precisely \\(n-1\\) non‑empty top strips.\n\nNow suppose a tile \\(R\\) contains cells from two different columns \\(j\\neq k\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(h_j>h_k\\).  \nSince column \\(k\\) has an uncovered square at row \\(r_k\\), the row interval of \\(R\\) must be contained in \\(\\{1,\\dots ,r_k-1\\}\\); otherwise \\(R\\) would contain the uncovered square \\((r_k,k)\\).  \nHence the height of \\(R\\) is at most \\(h_k\\).  \nAll cells of the taller strip \\(S_j^{\\text{top}}\\) that lie below row \\(h_k\\) (i.e. the rows \\(h_k+1,\\dots ,h_j\\)) remain uncovered by \\(R\\).  \nAny rectangle that tries to cover one of those remaining cells must also stay inside column \\(j\\) (otherwise it would intersect the uncovered square of column \\(k\\)).  \nThus the cells of \\(S_j^{\\text{top}}\\) cannot be shared with any other column; they require a tile dedicated to column \\(j\\).\n\nTherefore each non‑empty top strip forces a distinct tile.  \nHence any tiling uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles to cover the top part:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{at least }n-1\\text{ tiles are needed for the top strips.}}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Bottom strips  \n\nDefine the **bottom strip** of column \\(j\\) by  \n\n\\[\nS_j^{\\text{bottom}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid r_j< i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r_j=n\\) the strip is empty; otherwise it is a non‑empty rectangle.  \nIts height is  \n\n\\[\nb_j:=|S_j^{\\text{bottom}}|=n-r_j .\n\\]\n\nThe numbers \\(b_1,\\dots ,b_n\\) are also a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently the non‑zero heights are pairwise distinct, and exactly one column has \\(b_j=0\\) (the column whose uncovered square lies in the bottom row).  \nThus there are again \\(n-1\\) non‑empty bottom strips.\n\nThe same argument as in §3.1, applied to the bottom part of each column, shows that no two different bottom strips can be merged into a single tile.  Consequently any tiling needs at least another \\(n-1\\) tiles for the bottom part:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{at least }n-1\\text{ tiles are needed for the bottom strips.}}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  Combining the bounds  \n\nThe top strips and the bottom strips are disjoint (they occupy different rows), so the lower bounds (2) and (3) add.  Hence every admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Minimal number of tiles  \n\nFrom (1) we have a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and from §3 we have proved that no tiling can use fewer.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
