###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  The grid consists of the set of unit squares  \n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\((i,j)\\) denotes the square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(j\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Reduction to the diagonal  \n\nA placement of tiles together with the set of uncovered squares determines a bijection\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\n\\text{the uncovered square in row }i\\text{ is }(i,\\pi(i)).\n\\]\nPermuting rows by the permutation \\(\\sigma\\) and columns by \\(\\tau\\) replaces the uncovered\nset \\(\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\}\\) by \\(\\{(\\sigma(i),\\tau(\\pi(i)))\\}\\).  Choosing \\(\\sigma=\\tau\\) and\n\\(\\sigma(i)=\\pi(i)\\) sends the uncovered squares to the diagonal\n\\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).  Such a simultaneous permutation does not change the\nfeasibility of any tiling, therefore we may (and shall) assume that the uncovered\nsquares are exactly the diagonal cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of a tile  \n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; hence it is of the form  \n\\[\nR\\times C,\\qquad\nR=[r_1,r_2]\\cap\\mathbb Z,\\;C=[c_1,c_2]\\cap\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\nwith \\(1\\le r_1\\le r_2\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c_1\\le c_2\\le n\\).\nBecause no diagonal cell may be covered, the intervals \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) are disjoint.\nConsequently a tile is either  \n\n* **above the diagonal:** \\(r_2<c_1\\), or  \n* **below the diagonal:** \\(c_2<r_1\\).\n\nNo tile can contain cells from both sides of the diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound  \n\nConsider the cells just above the diagonal,\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\nEach \\((i,i+1)\\) lies in the upper‑diagonal region, so it must be covered by a tile\nlying above the diagonal.  Let such a tile have row interval \\(R=[a,b]\\) and column\ninterval \\(C=[c,d]\\) with \\(b<c\\).  For a cell \\((i,i+1)\\) to belong to this tile we need\n\\(i\\in R\\) and \\(i+1\\in C\\).  Because \\(c>b\\), the only possible index is \\(i=b\\) and\n\\(c=b+1\\).  Hence **an above‑diagonal tile can contain at most one element of \\(U\\)**,\nnamely \\((b,b+1)\\).\n\nSince \\(|U|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required above the diagonal.\n\nThe same argument applied to the cells just below the diagonal,\n\\[\nL=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\},\n\\]\nshows that each below‑diagonal tile can contain at most one element of \\(L\\), and\ntherefore at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required below the diagonal.\n\nTiles cannot be both above and below the diagonal, so any admissible tiling uses at\nleast\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.  This is a universal lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine the following \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.\n\n*For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)*\n\\[\nH_i=\\{i\\}\\times\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nis a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\).  It lies above the diagonal because\nall its columns exceed its row index \\(i\\).\n\n*For each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)*\n\\[\nV_i=\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{i\\}\n\\]\nis a vertical tile of size \\((n-i)\\times1\\).  It lies below the diagonal because all its\nrows exceed its column index \\(i\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{V_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint:\n* a horizontal tile uses only row \\(i\\), while a vertical tile uses only column \\(i\\);\n* the only common point of a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) and a vertical tile \\(V_i\\) would be\n\\((i,i)\\), which is not part of either rectangle.\n\nEvery off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs to exactly one of the rectangles:\n* if \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\);\n* if \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).\n\nThus the \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles cover **all** cells except the diagonal ones, and each row\nand each column has precisely the diagonal cell uncovered.  Hence a tiling with\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles proved in §3 is met by the construction of §4.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
