                "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. Let \\(n=2025\\). The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(p:[n]\\to[n]\\) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n2. Split the remaining squares into two disjoint sets  \n   \\[\n   A=\\{(i,p(j))\\mid i<j\\},\\qquad \n   B=\\{(i,p(j))\\mid i>j\\},\n   \\]\n   i.e. the squares strictly above and strictly below the permutation points.  \n3. Show (Lemma 1) that the \\(n-1\\) squares \\((i,p(i+1))\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) in \\(A\\) must lie in distinct tiles; similarly (Lemma 2) the \\(n-1\\) squares \\((i+1,p(i))\\) in \\(B\\) must lie in distinct tiles.  \n4. Prove (Lemma 3) that no single tile can contain a square from \\(A\\) and a square from \\(B\\) simultaneously. Hence at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles are required for any placement.  \n5. Provide a construction attaining this bound: choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (uncovered squares on the main diagonal). For each \\(i=1,\\dots,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering row \\(i\\) and columns \\(i+1,\\dots,n\\); for each \\(i=1,\\dots,n-1\\) place a vertical tile covering column \\(i\\) and rows \\(i+1,\\dots,n\\). This uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, covers every non‑diagonal square once, and respects all conditions.  \n6. Conclude that the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(p(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:[n]\\to[n]\\) is a bijection (a permutation).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Decomposition of the uncovered complement\n\nDefine two disjoint sets of squares\n\n\\[\nA=\\{(i,p(j))\\mid i<j\\},\\qquad \nB=\\{(i,p(j))\\mid i>j\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(A\\) (it lies strictly above the uncovered square in its column) or to \\(B\\) (it lies strictly below that uncovered square); thus the set of all covered squares is \\(A\\cup B\\) and \\(A\\cap B=\\varnothing\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two families of “critical’’ squares\n\nConsider the following two families of squares:\n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,p(i+1))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq A,\n\\qquad\nT=\\{(i+1,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq B .\n\\]\n\nBoth families contain exactly \\(n-1\\) squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lemma 1 – a tile contains at most one square of \\(S\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nIf a rectangular tile \\(R\\) (with row interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\)) contains two distinct squares of \\(S\\), say \\((i,p(i+1))\\) and \\((k,p(k+1))\\) with \\(i<k\\), then a contradiction arises.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\((i,p(i+1))\\in R\\), we have  \n\\(r_1\\le i\\le r_2\\) and \\(c_1\\le p(i+1)\\le c_2\\).  \nThe uncovered square in column \\(p(i+1)\\) is \\((i+1,p(i+1))\\).  \nSince column \\(p(i+1)\\) belongs to the column interval of \\(R\\), the row \\(i+1\\) cannot belong to the row interval; otherwise \\(R\\) would contain the uncovered square. Hence \\(r_2<i+1\\), i.e. \\(r_2\\le i\\).\n\nSimilarly, from \\((k,p(k+1))\\in R\\) we obtain \\(r_1\\le k\\le r_2\\) and \\(r_2\\le k\\).  \nThus \\(r_2\\ge k\\).\n\nCombining the two inequalities yields \\(i\\ge r_2\\ge k\\), contradicting \\(i<k\\). ∎\n\nConsequently each tile can cover **at most one** element of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are needed to cover all squares of \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Lemma 2 – a tile contains at most one square of \\(T\\)\n\nThe same argument applied to the set \\(T\\) (which is the image of \\(S\\) under the symmetry \\((i,j)\\mapsto(j,i)\\)) shows that a tile can contain at most one element of \\(T\\).  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all squares of \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Lemma 3 – a tile cannot contain squares from both \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)\n\n*Lemma.*  \nNo rectangular tile can contain simultaneously a square of \\(A\\) and a square of \\(B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a tile \\(R\\) with row interval \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) contains a square \\((i,p(j))\\) with \\(i<j\\) (hence in \\(A\\)) and a square \\((i',p(j'))\\) with \\(i'>j'\\) (hence in \\(B\\)).  \n\nBecause column \\(p(j)\\) belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\) and the uncovered square in that column is \\((j,p(j))\\), the row \\(j\\) cannot belong to \\([r_1,r_2]\\); therefore \\(r_2<j\\).  \n\nAnalogously, since column \\(p(j')\\) belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\) and the uncovered square in that column is \\((j',p(j'))\\), the row \\(j'\\) cannot belong to \\([r_1,r_2]\\); thus \\(r_1>j'\\).  \n\nConsequently\n\\[\nj' < r_1\\le r_2 < j .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf there existed any integer \\(k\\) with \\(j'<k<j\\), then the uncovered square \\((k,p(k))\\) would lie inside the rectangle \\(R\\): its row \\(k\\) satisfies (1) and its column \\(p(k)\\) lies between \\(p(j')\\) and \\(p(j)\\), hence belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\). This contradicts the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.  \n\nThus the interval \\((j',j)\\) contains no integer, which forces \\(j=j'+1\\).  \nBut then (1) becomes \\(j' < r_1\\le r_2 < j'+1\\), impossible because rows are integral.  \nHence such a tile cannot exist. ∎\n\nTherefore the tiles covering \\(A\\) and those covering \\(B\\) are disjoint families.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nFrom Lemmas 1–3 we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\