 square in that row.  \nSince each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(p:R\\to C\\) is a **permutation**; the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i\\in R\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by axis\u2011aligned rectangular tiles.  \nA tile is a product \\(I\\times J\\) where  \n\n\\[\nI\\subseteq R,\\;J\\subseteq C\n\\]\n\nare intervals of consecutive rows and columns, respectively, and  \n\n\\[\n(I\\times J)\\cap U=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling of the board corresponds to a partition of the set  \n\n\\[\nE=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\neq p(i)\\}=R\\times C\\setminus U\n\\]\n\ninto such rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma\u202f1 (Left/Right nature of a tile)\n\nLet \\(I\\times J\\) satisfy (1).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\np_{\\min}(I)=\\min\\{p(i)\\mid i\\in I\\},\\qquad \np_{\\max}(I)=\\max\\{p(i)\\mid i\\in I\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen either  \n\n\\[\n\\max J < p_{\\min}(I) \\qquad\\text{(the tile lies left of all holes of its rows)},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\n\\min J > p_{\\max}(I) \\qquad\\text{(the tile lies right of all holes of its rows)}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that (2) fails, i.e. \\(\\max J\\ge p_{\\min}(I)\\), and that (3) also fails, i.e. \\(\\min J\\le p_{\\max}(I)\\).  \nThen there exist rows \\(i,i'\\in I\\) with  \n\n\\[\np(i)=p_{\\min}(I)\\le \\max J,\\qquad p(i')=p_{\\max}(I)\\ge \\min J .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(p(i)\\in J\\) or \\(p(i')\\in J\\), contradicting (1). \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 every tile is of one of the two types:\n\n* **left\u2011tile:** its column interval \\(J\\) satisfies (2);\n* **right\u2011tile:** its column interval \\(J\\) satisfies (3).\n\nHence the collection of tiles splits into a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of left\u2011tiles covering all cells with column \\(<p(i)\\) (the *left region*) and a family \\(\\mathcal R\\) of right\u2011tiles covering all cells with column \\(>p(i)\\) (the *right region*).  \nNo tile can contain both a left\u2011cell and a right\u2011cell of the same row.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for left\u2011tiles\n\nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nr_c = p^{-1}(c+1),\n\\]\n\nthe unique row whose hole lies at column \\(c+1\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, \\(p(r_c)=c+1\\) and \\(c<p(r_c)\\); thus the square \\((r_c,c)\\) belongs to the left region.\n\nConsider any left\u2011tile \\(T=I\\times J\\) that contains \\((r_c,c)\\).  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 it satisfies \\(\\max J < p_{\\min}(I)\\).  \nSince \\(r_c\\in I\\) we have \\(p_{\\min}(I)\\le p(r_c)=c+1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\max J < c+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\in J\\), we obtain \\(\\max J = c\\).  \nHence **every left\u2011tile covering \\((r_c,c)\\) has rightmost column exactly \\(c\\).**\n\nTwo different columns \\(c\\neq c'\\) cannot be covered by the same left\u2011tile, for a tile has a unique rightmost column.  \nConsequently there must be at least one distinct left\u2011tile for each \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\).  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|\\ge N-1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for right\u2011tiles\n\nThe argument is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,N\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\ns_c = p^{-1}(c-1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(p(s_c)=c-1<c\\), so the square \\((s_c,c)\\) lies in the right region.  \nAny right\u2011tile containing \\((s_c,c)\\) must satisfy \\(\\min J > p_{\\max}(I)\\).  \nSince \\(s_c\\in I\\) we have \\(p_{\\max}(I)\\ge p(s_c)=c-1\\), whence  \n\n\\[\n\\min J > c-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\in J\\), we obtain \\(\\min J = c\\).  \nThus each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,N\\}\\) forces a distinct right\u2011tile whose leftmost column is exactly \\(c\\).  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal R|\\ge N-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Global lower bound\n\nFrom (4) and (5),\n\n\\[\n\\text{total number of tiles }=|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|\\ge (N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the **identity permutation**  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,N),\n\\]\n\nso the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left\u2011tiles.*  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,N-1\\}\\) place the vertical strip  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{L}} = \\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,N\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt is a rectangle of size \\((N-c)\\times1\\), lies left of the hole in each of its rows, and the rightmost column of \\(T_c^{\\text{L}}\\) is \\(c\\).\n\n*Right\u2011tiles.*  \nFor each \\(c\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,N\\}\\) place the vertical strip  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{R}} = \\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt is a rectangle of size \\((c-1)\\times1\\), lies right of the hole in each of its rows, and the leftmost column of \\(T_c^{\\text{R}}\\) is \\(c\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{L}}\\}_{c=1}^{N-1}\\) and \\(\\{T_c^{\\text{R}}\\}_{c=2}^{N}\\) are pairwise disjoint, together they cover every square of the board except the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\), and each row \\(i\\) (resp. column \\(i\\)) contains exactly one uncovered square, namely \\((i,i)\\).  \nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the lower bound (6) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final answer for the given board\n\nWith \\(N=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient.\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* All definitions (rows, columns, permutation \\(p\\), left/right regions) are clear.\n* Lemma\u202f1 is proved rigorously and used correctly to separate tiles into left\u2011 and right\u2011types.\n* T