ectangle is a biclique). So lower bound holds: any tiling yields a biclique partition, so the minimal number of rectangles is at least the biclique partition number. So lower bound 2N-2 is valid if the biclique partition number is indeed >=2N-2.\n\nNow we need to verify the lower bound argument: They used Boolean rank of matrix A = J - I (all ones except zeros on diagonal). They claim that the Boolean rank of A is at least 2N-2 using a fooling set S = {(i,i+1) and (i+1,i)} for i=1..N-1. They argue each rank-1 binary matrix can contain at most one element of S. Let's check that.\n\nDefine S = { (i,i+1) for i=1..N-1 } ∪ { (i+1,i) for i=1..N-1 }. So S has 2(N-1) = 2N-2 entries. They claim that any rank-1 binary matrix (outer product uv^T) cannot contain two distinct positions from S. Let's examine: A rank-1 binary matrix is of the form u_i * v_j where u_i, v_j ∈ {0,1}. So the matrix has ones exactly at positions where u_i=1 and v_j=1. So the support is a rectangle: product of row set R' = {i | u_i=1} and column set C' = {j | v_j=1}. So any rank-1 binary matrix corresponds to a rectangle (not necessarily contiguous). So the claim is that any rectangle cannot contain two entries from S. Let's test: Suppose we have two entries (a,b) and (c,d) from S. The rectangle must contain all rows in {a,c} and all columns in {b,d}. So the rectangle includes positions (a,b), (a,d), (c,b), (c,d). If (a,b) and (c,d) are both in S, then the rectangle also includes (a,d) and (c,b). The claim is that at least one of those is a diagonal entry (i,i) which is zero in A. Indeed, S consists of off-diagonal entries adjacent to diagonal: (i,i+1) and (i+1,i). Let's examine possible pairs: If we pick (a,b) = (i,i+1) and (c,d) = (j,j+1) for i≠j, then rectangle includes (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Are either of those diagonal? Not necessarily. But the claim says one of (a,d) or (c,b) is a diagonal entry. Let's test with i=1, j=3: (1,2) and (3,4). Then (a,d) = (1,4) not diagonal; (c,b) = (3,2) not diagonal. 