e each rectangle is a biclique). So lower bound holds: any tiling yields a biclique partition, so the minimal number of rectangles is at least the biclique partition number. So lower bound 2N-2 is valid if the biclique partition number is indeed >=2N-2.\n\nNow we need to verify the lower bound argument: They used Boolean rank of matrix A = J - I (all ones except zeros on diagonal). They claim that the Boolean rank of A is at least 2N-2 using a fooling set S = {(i,i+1) and (i+1,i)} for i=1..N-1. They argue each rank-1 binary matrix can contain at most one element of S. Let's check that.\n\nDefine S = { (i,i+1) for i=1..N-1 } ∪ { (i+1,i) for i=1..N-1 }. So S has 2(N-1) = 2N-2 entries. They claim that any rank-1 binary matrix (outer product uv^T) cannot contain two distinct positions from S. Let's examine: A rank-1 binary matrix is of the form u_i * v_j where u_i, v_j ∈ {0,1}. So the matrix has ones exactly at positions where u_i=1 and v_j=1. So the support is a rectangle: product of row set R' = {i | u_i=1} and column set C' = {j | v_j=1}. So any rank-1 binary matrix corresponds to a rectangle (not necessarily contiguous). So the claim is that any rectangle cannot contain two entries from S. Let's test: Suppose we have two entries (a,b) and (c,d) from S. The rectangle must contain all rows in {a,c} and all columns in {b,d}. So the rectangle includes positions (a,b), (a,d), (c,b), (c,d). If (a,b) and (c,d) are both in S, then the rectangle also includes (a,d) and (c,b). The claim is that at least one of those is a diagonal entry (i,i) which is zero in A. Indeed, S consists of off-diagonal entries adjacent to diagonal: (i,i+1) and (i+1,i). Let's examine possible pairs: If we pick (a,b) = (i,i+1) and (c,d) = (j,j+1) for i≠j, then rectangle includes (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Are either of those diagonal? Not necessarily. But the claim says one of (a,d) or (c,b) is a diagonal entry. Let's test with i=1, j=3: (1,2) and (3,4). Then (a,d) = (1,4) not diagonal; (c,b) = (3,2) not diagonal. So the claim is false for that pair. However, the claim might be that any two distinct entries of S cannot be covered by a rank-1 matrix because the rectangle would contain a diagonal zero entry. But we need to check all possible pairs.\n\nActually, the rectangle defined by rows {i,i+1} and columns {i+1,i} (i.e., one entry from each part of S: (i,i+1) and (i+1,i)). That rectangle includes (i,i) and (i+1,i+1) which are diagonal zeros. So that pair cannot be covered. But if we pick two entries from S that are not \"paired\" like (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) where j ≠ i, the rectangle includes (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Those are not diagonal. However, the rectangle also includes (i,i+1) and (j,j+1). So that rectangle would have ones at those positions, but also includes other positions (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). Are those allowed? The rectangle must be a subset of the ones of A (i.e., cannot include any zeros). The zeros of A are the diagonal entries (i,i). So the rectangle can include any off-diagonal entries, including (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). So that rectangle would be allowed as a rank-1 matrix covering both (i,i+1) and (j,j+1). But the rectangle also includes (i,i+1) and (j,j+1) and also includes (i,j+1) and (j,i+1). All those are off-diagonal entries (since i≠j+1 and j≠i+1). So the rectangle does not include any diagonal zeros. So it seems a rank-1 matrix can cover two entries from S that are not \"adjacent\" pairs. So the claim that each rank-1 matrix can cover at most one element of S is false. Let's examine the set S more carefully: S = { (i,i+1) for i=1..N-1 } ∪ { (i+1,i) for i=1..N-1 }. So S includes pairs (i,i+1) and (i+1,i) for each i. So there are N-1 pairs of \"adjacent\" off-diagonal entries symmetric across diagonal. The claim that any rank-1 matrix can cover at most one element of S might be false if we can pick two entries from S that are not \"adjacent\" in the sense that they are not in the same row or column? Let's test: Suppose we pick (1,2) and (3,4). The recta