                "provider_specific_fields": {
                    "reasoning": "We have a problem: \"Consider a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of different sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nSolution provided: They define N=2025, R and C sets. They argue uncovered squares form a permutation; each row has exactly one uncovered unit square, each column also exactly one, so uncovered squares are a permutation. Then they interpret tiles as bicliques in a bipartite graph G = K_{N,N} minus the perfect matching of uncovered squares. They then lower bound the biclique partition number of G using Boolean rank of matrix A = J - I (or J - P). They define a fooling set S of size 2N-2, show each rank-1 binary matrix can cover at most one element of S, thus Boolean rank >= 2N-2, thus biclique partition number >= 2N-2, thus any tiling uses at least 2N-2 tiles.\n\nThen they give a construction achieving 2N-2 tiles: choose identity permutation (uncovered squares are diagonal). For each column j, place vertical rectangle above diagonal (rows 1..j-1) and vertical rectangle below diagonal (rows j+1..N). That yields (N-1)+(N-1) = 2N-2 rectangles, covering all cells except diagonal. So answer is 2N-2 = 4048.\n\nWe need to evaluate solution according to hierarchical decision tree and the extra constraints about construction claims.\n\nFirst, Level 1: answer correctness. The answer is 4048. Is that correct? We need to check if the answer is indeed minimal. The solution provides a lower bound and a construction achieving it, so answer seems correct. But we need to verify if the lower bound is valid. The solution uses a known result: biclique partition number of K_{N,N} minus a perfect matching is at least 2N-2. They prove using a fooling set S of size 2N-2. The argument seems plausible. The construction indeed uses 2N-2 tiles. So answer is correct.\n\nThus Level 1: answer correctness = CORRECT.\n\nLevel 2: reasoning validity. Need to check if the solution uses valid mathematical methods. The solution uses combinatorial reasoning, graph theory, Boolean rank, fooling set lower bound. All are valid methods. However, we need to check the \"construction claims\" for Level 2: According to the enhanced rule, for FIND problems, we need to check if the solution provides any concrete details for the construction. The solution claims a construction: For each column j with 2 <= j <= N, place vertical rectangle R_up_j = {(i,j) | 1 <= i <= j-1}. For each column j with 1 <= j <= N-1, place vertical rectangle R_down_j = {(i,j) | j+1 <= i <= N}. This is explicit: they give the set of coordinates for each rectangle. That's concrete detail: they provide formulas for each rectangle. So this is Category C (explicit specification). Thus Level 2 passes: methods are valid and construction claims have concrete details.\n\nThus Level 2: reasoning uses VALID methods, passes.\n\nNow Level 3: presentation quality. Are there any justification gaps or critical errors? Let's examine the solution.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- The lower bound proof uses a lemma about a fooling set S = {(i,i+1)} ∪ {(i+1,i)} for i=1..N-1. They claim each rank-1 binary matrix can cover at most one element of S. The proof: they take two distinct entries (a,b) and (c,d) in S. Then the outer product of characteristic vectors of rows {a,c} and columns {b,d} would contain four positions (a,b), (a,d), (c,b), (c,d). Because the two chosen entries belong to S, one of the pairs (a,d) or (c,b) is a diagonal entry (i,i). Indeed, S consists of entries just above and below the diagonal. If you pick two entries from S, they could be both above diagonal, both below, or one above and one below. Let's test: Suppose (a,b) = (i,i+1) (above diagonal). Then